/* 段落记忆 · 词层 2 · 子块 a：词头 → {tw:[关系词,关系类型], en, zh} */
window.APP_MERGE_NET(2,{"sub":"a","par":{"account":{"tw":[["accountable",4],["bill",0],["charge",3],["inform",2],["value",6],["accountant",4],["history",0],["check",3],["news",2],["interest",6],["report",0]],"en":"My sister works as an accountant in a small office, and she says every account tells a story. When a bill came with a charge we did not expect, she had to inform the manager at once. She checked the whole history of payments herself, because she is the one who is accountable for the numbers. The value of each entry had to be right, and she would not sign the report until it was. Later we read the news that the bank had raised its interest again. She laughed and said she would check her own account first. It had been a long day at the desk.","zh":"我姐姐在一家小事务所做会计，她说每一本账都讲着一个故事。有一回账单上多出一笔我们没料到的费用，她立刻就得通知经理。她自己把整份付款记录都查了一遍，因为对数字负责的人就是她。每一笔的金额都得对，报告不改对她是不会签字的。后来我们看到新闻，说银行又把利息提了。她笑了笑说，还是先查查自己的账户吧。在桌前坐了整整一天，够长的。"},"apply":{"tw":[["applicable",4],["employ",0],["dress",3],["cover",2],["permit",6],["applicant",4],["hold",0],["follow",3],["refer",2],["allow",6],["use",0]],"en":"The company wanted to employ two new clerks for the summer, so I helped read every application. One applicant had written that the rule was not applicable to her case, and she asked us to allow her more time. The manager would not permit that. He said each person must dress neatly, cover the desk when someone was away, and follow the same routine. I had to refer her letter to the head office. They hold the final word. She could still use the old form if she wished to apply again. No one in the room liked that answer, least of all me.","zh":"公司想为夏天雇两名新职员，于是我也帮着看了每一份申请。有位申请者写道，那条规矩不适用于她的情况，还请求我们多给她些时间。经理不同意。他说每个人都得穿得整洁，别人不在时要顶住柜台，而且照同一套流程走。我只好把她的信转给总部。最后拍板的是他们。她要是还想再申请，用旧表格也行。屋里没人喜欢这个答复，最不喜欢的就是我。"},"attend":{"tw":[["attention",4],["hang",0],["tend",3],["care",2],["miss",1],["participate",6],["attentive",4],["look",0],["minister",3],["help",2],["visit",6]],"en":"Every morning the students attend a short meeting in the hall, and they must pay attention while the head reads the notices. My cousin tends to hang at the back with her friends. She does not care much for rules, but she is attentive when a real story begins. Last week a minister came to visit the school and asked who would participate in the reading week. She raised her hand first. He said he would help any pupil who needed a look at the books before the test, and that no one should miss the chance. Outside, the bell rang for the first class.","zh":"每天早上学生们都要去礼堂开一个短会，校长念通知的时候必须专心听。我表妹总爱跟朋友一起在后排晃着。她不太在乎规矩，可一旦有真正的故事开始，她就听得很入神。上周有位牧师来学校访问，问谁愿意参加阅读周。她第一个举了手。他说，谁想在考试前翻翻那些书，他都愿意帮忙，这个机会谁都不该错过。外面，第一节课的铃响了。"},"contract":{"tw":[["contraction",4],["cut",0],["catch",3],["change",2],["expand",1],["deal",6],["contractual",4],["press",0],["rent",3],["promise",2],["lease",6]],"en":"The old printing press had been in the family for years, but the shop could not expand without a new contract. My uncle signed a lease for the room next door, though the rent was high. The deal had one clause he failed to catch, and it turned the whole thing into a contractual trap. He had to cut his hours and promise the owner a share of the profits. The paper showed a strange contraction in the market that year, and the terms might change again. Still, he pressed on. The machine still runs every morning.","zh":"那台老印刷机在家里放了好多年，可铺子想扩大就得签新合同。我叔租下了隔壁那间房，租金不低。这笔交易里有一条他没看出来的条款，结果整件事变成了合同上的陷阱。他只好缩短工时，还答应给房东一份利润。那年报纸上说市场出现了一种奇怪的收缩，条件说不定还会再变。可他照样硬撑着。每天早上，那台机器还在转。"},"capital":{"tw":[["capitalist",4],["great",0],["stock",3],["book",2],["northern",6],["capitalise",4],["cap",0],["principal",3],["seat",2],["east",6]],"en":"The company needed fresh capital before winter, so its principal owner went to the stock market with a bold plan. He hoped to capitalise on the new demand for warm coats and to open a second shop in the northern part of the capital. A capitalist friend agreed to put money in the book, but he wanted a seat on the board. My father, who works at the great old factory in the east, read about it in the paper. He put on his flat cap and laughed. He said the money would come and go, but the town would stay.","zh":"公司入冬前得筹一笔新资本，于是大股东带着一个大胆的计划去了股市。他想借保暖外套的新需求大赚一笔，还想在首都北边再开一家店。一个有钱的朋友答应出资入账，但要一个董事的位子。我爸在东边那家了不起的老厂上班，在报上读到了这事。他戴上那顶扁帽笑了，说钱会来来去去，可这座镇子会留下来。"},"contain":{"tw":[["content",4],["bear",0],["catch",3],["include",2],["material",6],["continent",4],["carry",0],["train",3],["produce",6],["check",0]],"en":"The new box was built to contain the powder safely, and its contents included a small metal part. The material had to bear heat and cold without cracking. It could carry the load by train across the whole continent. I had to check the seal twice before I let it go. Each unit would produce a fine dust if the lid came loose, and the lab would catch the mistake at once. The manager said the design was sound, and he seemed content with it. We loaded it that afternoon, and nothing spilled on the floor. The team clapped once, then went back to work.","zh":"这个新箱子是为了安全装那种粉末造的，里面的东西还包括一个小金属件。材料得经得住冷热而不裂。它能靠火车载着这批货穿过整个大陆。放行之前，我把封口查了两遍。盖子一松，每个单元都会产生细粉，实验室马上就能发现这个错。经理说设计没问题，看样子他也挺满意。那天下午我们把它装上了车，什么都没洒到地上。大家鼓了一下掌，又回去干活了。"},"appeal":{"tw":[["appellate",4],["attract",0],["suit",3],["mention",2],["court",6],["charm",0],["capture",3],["challenge",2],["supreme",6],["invoke",0]],"en":"The old theatre had lost its charm, and the owner made a public appeal for help to save it. The story would attract many readers, and my editor asked me to mention it in the evening news. A lawyer said the case might go to a higher court, and even reach the supreme bench on appeal. An appellate judge could capture the whole question in one line. The owner faced a challenge: in a dark suit, with no money and no plan. He would invoke the name of the town itself. I wrote it down and hoped someone would answer. By Friday a few strangers had answered.","zh":"那座老戏院没了往日的魅力，老板公开呼吁大家帮一把把它保住。这事会吸引很多读者，编辑叫我在晚间新闻里提一提。一位律师说，这案子也许要上诉到更高一级的法院，甚至递到最高法院。上诉法官一句话就能把整件事说清。老板面临着难题：一身深色西装，既没钱也没主意。他要搬出这座镇子的名字来说话。我把这些都记了下来，盼着有人回应。到周五，还真有几个陌生人回应了。"},"benefit":{"tw":[["beneficial",4],["gain",0],["advantage",3],["good",2],["raise",6],["profit",0],["net",3],["help",2],["contribute",6],["welfare",0]],"en":"The small factory offered its workers one clear benefit: a free lunch at noon. My mother said the real advantage was the time it gave her, and the net gain in her pay after the new raise. She believed that a short rest was beneficial for everyone on the floor. Each worker had to contribute a little to the welfare fund, which would help anyone who fell ill. She could see no profit in working through the break. It was simply good sense, she said, and she would not give it up for anything. The others on the line agreed with her.","zh":"这家小厂给工人一项实实在在的好处：中午免费供一顿饭。我妈说真正的好处是它能给自己腾出时间，还有加薪之后到手的净增。她相信，歇一小会儿对车间里的人都有益。每个工人都得往福利金里交一点，谁病了就能帮上忙。她觉得不歇午休硬撑着干活毫无收益。她说这就是个常识问题，谁说什么她也不肯放弃。流水线上其他人都同意她。"},"cast":{"tw":[["caster",4],["cat",0],["press",3],["move",2],["picture",6],["draw",0],["shoot",3],["send",2],["film",6],["drop",0]],"en":"The whole cast gathered on the first morning to shoot the opening scene of the film. The director had to move fast, because the light would change by noon. He asked the young caster to send the script to the press, and to draw a rough picture of the set. A grey cat walked across the floor and refused to drop the paper it had found. We had to wait, then start again. After lunch the crew packed up and went home, tired but happy, with the first take safely in the box. It was a good beginning, and no one complained.","zh":"第一天早上，全体演员聚齐了，要拍片子开头那场戏。导演得抓紧，因为到中午光线就变了。他让那位年轻的选角人把剧本发给报社，再随手画一张布景的草图。一只灰猫走过地板，叼着它捡到的纸就是不肯撒口。我们只好等，然后再来一遍。午饭后剧组收拾东西回家，又累又高兴，第一个镜头稳稳地收进了箱子里。开头不错，谁都没抱怨。"},"circle":{"tw":[["circulate",4],["lot",0],["party",3],["shape",2],["edge",6],["round",0],["loop",3],["travel",2],["parallel",6],["band",0]],"en":"At the party the guests stood in a wide circle, and a small band played near the door. My aunt held a round plate and moved along the edge of the room, saying hello to everyone. She said a story like that would travel fast once it began to circulate. Two men walked on parallel lines, neither of them listening. In the parking lot outside, a boy rode his bike in a slow loop, over and over. Someone had drawn a strange shape on the wall with chalk. It was, my aunt said, just another evening. The music stopped at ten.","zh":"晚会上客人们围成一个很大的圆圈，门边有一支小乐队在演奏。我姑妈端着一只圆盘子，沿着屋子边走动，跟每个人打招呼。她说这种故事一旦传开，跑得飞快。两个男人一前一后走着平行的两条线，谁也没在听。外面的停车场上，一个男孩骑着车一圈一圈地绕。有人用粉笔在墙上画了个怪形状。我姑妈说，又是一个寻常的夜晚罢了。音乐十点就停了。"},"counter":{"tw":[["count",4],["return",0],["bar",3],["act",2],["crack",6],["buffet",0],["chip",3],["answer",2],["response",6],["retort",0]],"en":"At the buffet the girl behind the counter had to count every coin twice before she gave change. A man wanted to return a cracked cup he had bought that morning. She asked for his receipt, but his answer was only a sharp retort. He began to act as if the whole shop were against him. Beside him a bar of chocolate and a bag of chips lay on the shelf. Her quiet response did not change his mood. She let him go without a word, then went back to her work. It had been a long and slow afternoon at the shop.","zh":"自助餐台后面的姑娘找零前得把每一枚硬币数两遍。一个男人想退掉早上买的那只有裂纹的杯子。她要看收据，他的回答却只是一句尖刻的反驳。他开始闹起来，好像整家店都跟他作对。他旁边的架子上放着一块巧克力和一袋薯片。她平静的回应没能改变他的情绪。她没再多说，任他走了，然后回去做自己的事。这个小店的下午，又长又慢。"},"crack":{"tw":[["cracker",4],["break",2],["fault",3],["change",2],["curb",6],["check",3],["split",3],["hit",2],["stop",6],["offer",0]],"en":"One cold night we heard a loud crack from the old stone step outside the door. My father went out to check it and found a long split running across the surface. The fault was not in the stone but in the ground beneath it. The step had been hit hard that winter. He said the frost would break it further unless we did something. He put a curb of new boards along the edge to stop the damage, and then offered my mother a cracker and a cup of tea. She said the change in the weather had done this, and he agreed.","zh":"一个寒冷的夜里，我们听见门外那级老石阶啪地响了一声。我爸出去查看，发现一道长裂缝横在阶面上。问题不在石头，而在下面的地面。那个冬天，这级石阶被冻得够呛。他说再这么下去霜会把石头冻裂得更厉害。他沿着边加了一道新木板做挡沿，止住了损坏，然后拿了一块饼干和一杯茶给我妈。她说这都是天气变了闹的，他也这么认为。"},"assume":{"tw":[["assumption",4],["accept",0],["hat",3],["change",2],["bear",0],["play",3],["dress",2],["strike",0]],"en":"When my sister did not come down for supper, my father made a quiet assumption: she was angry with us. He would not accept that she might simply be tired. He put on his old hat and went to the foot of the stairs. I could not bear the silence, so I began to play a record softly. She appeared at last in a plain dress, and the change in her face made us both laugh. She said I should not assume things so quickly. Then the clock began to strike eight, and we all sat down together. It was a strange evening.","zh":"我妹妹没下来吃晚饭，我爸心里就悄悄认定：她在跟我们生气。他不肯接受她也许只是累了。他戴上旧帽子，走到楼梯口。我受不了那份静，就轻轻放起了一张唱片。她终于穿着一身素裙子下来了，脸上那点变化让我们俩都笑了。她说我不该这么快就下结论。接着钟敲了八下，我们一齐坐下吃饭。那是个有点奇怪的晚上。"},"creature":{"tw":[["create",4],["animal",0],["giant",3],["organism",2],["alien",6],["beast",0],["pet",3],["monster",6],["fauna",0]],"en":"On a slow walk through the forest, my cousin liked to name every creature we met. She said each small organism had its own way of living, and that even a giant tree could not create a home as strange as a pond. A shy animal crossed the path and vanished. She laughed and called it a little monster, then admitted it was only a pet of the woods. We talked about alien worlds and the fauna of other planets. Then a real beast, a deer, stepped out into the light and stood still for us. It was there for a long moment.","zh":"在林子里慢悠悠地走着，我表妹喜欢给我们遇见的每只生物起名字。她说每种微小的生物都有自己活着的法子，连一棵大树也造不出像池塘那样奇特的家。一只胆小的动物穿过小路，一晃就不见了。她笑着叫它小怪物，随后又承认它不过是这片林子的小宠物。我们聊起外星的天地和别的星球上的动物群。这时一头真正的野兽——一只鹿——走进光里，为我们停住了。它在那儿站了好一会儿。"},"advance":{"tw":[["further",0],["carry",3],["better",2],["back",1],["reach",6],["raise",0],["edge",3],["move",2],["ahead",6],["rise",0]],"en":"With every step we tried to advance a little further along the ridge. My father would carry the heavy bag and walk just ahead of me. He said a better plan was to move slowly and to reach the top before dark. The path ran along a sharp edge, and I did not want to look back. I had to raise my hand once to keep my balance. We could see the sun rise over the far hills, and it lifted my mood. In the end the climb did not take as long as I had feared. It was worth it.","zh":"每走一步，我们都想沿着山脊再往前挪一点。我爸背着沉包，走在我前头一点。他说更好的办法是慢慢走，天黑前赶到山顶。小路贴着一条陡峭的边，我不想回头。有一回我不得不抬起手来保持平衡。我们能看见太阳从远处的山头上爬起来，我的心情也跟着轻快了。到头来这段爬升没我担心的那么久。值了。"},"associate":{"tw":[["fellow",0],["company",3],["think",2],["dissociate",1],["graduate",6],["relate",0],["date",3],["unite",2],["adjunct",6],["connect",0]],"en":"At the reunion I met a former fellow student, and I began to associate his face with a cold morning long ago. We talked about old times. He now worked for a large company, and he said the job had taught him to relate to all kinds of people. We tried to connect each name to a face and to date the year we had left. I think he had changed more than I had. He could not dissociate his old self from the man he was now. A graduate from the year above joined us, and the three of us seemed to unite over one small memory. He was an adjunct teacher then, still finding his way.","zh":"聚会上我遇到一位当年的同学，我开始把他的脸和很久以前一个寒冷的早晨连在一起。我们聊起了旧时光。他现在在一家大公司做事，说这份工作教会他怎么跟各种各样的人打交道。我们试着把每个名字对上脸，再推算是哪一年散的。我觉得他比我变得多。他没法把当年的自己和现在的自己分开。一位高一届的毕业生也凑了过来，我们三个好像因为一点小事就聚成了一伙。那时他还是个兼职教员，还在摸自己的路。"},"attach":{"tw":[["tie",0],["couple",3],["join",2],["detach",1],["enlarge",6],["bind",3],["tape",3],["relate",2],["remove",6],["bond",0]],"en":"My mother had to attach a name card to every parcel before the fair. She would tie a thin string, join the two ends, and bind the whole thing with brown tape. A couple of boxes were too heavy, so she asked me to remove the old labels first. She wanted to enlarge the picture on the front page and to relate it to the theme of the year. If a card would not stay, she could detach it and start again. By evening every box had a small bond of string and paper that held it closed. It was quiet work.","zh":"展销会之前，我妈得给每个包裹都系上一张姓名卡。她会打一个细绳结，把两头接上，再用棕色胶带把整个绑好。有两个箱子太沉，她叫我把旧标签先撕下来。她还想把首页的图放大，让它和今年的主题对得上。卡片要是挂不住，她就把它解下来重来。到傍晚，每个箱子都有一小股绳子和纸把它封住了。这活儿很安静。"},"attack":{"tw":[["fire",3],["charge",3],["act",2],["defend",1],["suicide",6],["round",0],["check",3],["knock",2],["raid",6],["approach",0]],"en":"The morning news told of an attack on a small shop near the river. Someone had set a fire at the back door, and the police made a charge against two men the next day. The owner had to act fast to defend his family, and he ran a quick check of the whole street. Later there was a knock at his door, and an officer came to ask about the raid. Some in the crowd spoke of a suicide, but the paper said that was not true. The reporter had to approach the story with care. In the first round of questions, nobody gave a clear answer.","zh":"早间新闻说河边一家小店遭了袭击。有人在店后门放了火，第二天警方对两名男子提出了指控。店主只得赶紧护住家人，还飞快地把整条街查看了一遍。后来有人敲他家的门，一位警官来问那场突袭的事。人群里有人提到自杀，可报纸说根本不是这么回事。记者只得小心翼翼地靠近这桩新闻。头一轮问下来，谁也没给出个明白的答复。"},"bounce":{"tw":[["spring",0],["skip",3],["hit",2],["clear",1],["slip",6],["leap",0],["pounce",3],["jump",2],["knock",6],["rebound",0]],"en":"The children played with an old ball near the wall, and it would bounce off the stones again and again. One boy tried to leap up and catch it, but his foot began to slip. He took a short jump, missed, and the ball hit him on the arm. His sister ran to pounce on it before it rolled away. She said the ball had a strong spring inside. The little ones began to skip, and the ball had to rebound off the wall for them. Then someone gave the door a loud knock, and a neighbour asked them to clear the street. After that the game went on, but with less noise.","zh":"孩子们在墙边玩一个旧球，球一次次从石头上弹回来。一个男孩想跳起来接住它，脚却开始打滑。他短短地蹦了一下，没够着，球砸在他胳膊上。他妹妹赶紧扑过去，趁球滚远前把它按住。她说这球里头弹力足。小家伙们开始蹦蹦跳跳，球还得替他们从墙上弹回来。这时有人重重地敲了一下门，一位邻居让他们把街面让开。打那以后，游戏接着玩，只是没那么吵了。"},"calm":{"tw":[["quiet",5],["compose",3],["comfort",2],["agitate",1],["tense",6],["steady",0],["repose",3],["wind",2],["stay",6],["serene",0]],"en":"After the storm the sea turned calm, and a light wind moved over the water. The fishing boats stayed close to the shore, and their owners tried to compose themselves after a tense night. One old man said the quiet of the morning was a comfort to him. He would not let the fear agitate his mind, and he kept his voice steady. He found a kind of repose in watching the grey waves. He told us to stay on the sand and to keep our faces serene. Nobody spoke for a long time. That morning the whole bay was at peace.","zh":"风暴过后，海面平静下来，一阵轻风从水上拂过。渔船都停在岸边，船主们经过一夜的紧张，正努力让自己定下神来。一位老人说，早晨这份安静让他心里好受了些。他不让恐惧搅乱自己的心，声音始终很稳。他望着灰白的浪，心也跟着安了下来。他叫我们待在沙上，脸色放平静些。好一阵没人说话。那天早上，整个海湾都安安静静的。"},"claim":{"tw":[["call",0],["charge",3],["right",2],["forfeit",1],["exact",0],["demand",3],["affirm",2],["legitimate",6],["title",0]],"en":"The family made a claim on the old house, and the court had to decide who held the title. Their lawyer would call the neighbour and demand the exact date of the sale. He said the claim was legitimate and would not forfeit it for anything. The other side tried to charge the family with a lie, but could not affirm it with any paper. In the end the judge gave them the right to stay one more year. There was no shout of joy in the room, only a long breath. It was, in the end, a quiet and fair ending.","zh":"这家人对那栋老房子提出了主张，法庭得判定产权归谁。他们的律师会传唤邻居，要求说出买卖的确切日期。他说这项主张是正当的，说什么也不会放弃。对方想指控这家人撒谎，却拿不出任何纸面证据来证实。最后法官判他们可以再住一年。屋里没有欢呼，只有长长的一口气。到头来，这是个安静而公道的结局。"},"commit":{"tw":[["committee",4],["charge",0],["use",2],["guilty",6],["commission",4],["send",0],["criminal",6]],"en":"The committee met to decide what to do with the money, and each member had to commit to one clear plan. The chair read the charge against a former worker who was said to be guilty of taking tools. He had used the workshop after hours, and the group had to send a report to the head office. Someone said the man was no criminal, only careless. A small commission was set up to look into it. In the end they voted to give him one more chance and to keep the matter quiet. Nobody in the room talked about it again.","zh":"委员会开会商量这笔钱怎么办，每位成员都得对一套明确的方案做出承诺。主席念了对一名前员工的指控，据说他拿走了工具，是有过失的。他曾在工余时间用过车间，小组只得给总部送一份报告。有人说这人不是什么罪犯，只是不小心。于是成立了一个小组去查这事。最后他们投票决定再给他一次机会，把事情压下不提。屋里再没人谈起过。"},"credit":{"tw":[["mention",0],["believe",3],["note",2],["cash",1],["debt",6],["cite",0],["finance",3],["trust",2],["mortgage",6],["recognition",0]],"en":"The bank would not give my uncle more credit until he paid off the old debt. He had to mention the new job in his letter and cite the paper that showed his income. The clerk made a note about the mortgage and asked how much cash he could bring each month. He said he did not believe in borrowing, but he needed trust from the bank before the finance office would help. He wanted no special recognition, only a fair chance. In the end they agreed to review his file in spring. It was slow and careful work, but hopeful.","zh":"银行不肯再给我叔贷款，除非他把旧债还清。他得在信里提到新工作，还要引用那份证明收入的材料。职员在抵押贷款那栏做了记录，问他每月能拿出多少现金。他说他并不相信借债，可在财务部门帮忙之前，他得先得到银行的信任。他不要什么特别的认可，只求一个公道的机会。最后他们答应春天再看他那份材料。这活儿又慢又细，可总算有盼头。"},"anger":{"tw":[["angry",4],["wrath",0],["steam",3],["feel",2],["outrage",6],["rage",3],["emotion",2],["fear",6]],"en":"When the letter came, a cold anger rose in my father's face, and for a moment he could not speak. His wrath was quiet, which was worse than shouting. He was angry, but he kept it inside. He stood by the stove and watched the steam climb from the pot. I could feel the same emotion in my own chest, mixed with a little fear. The news of the broken promise had caused a real outrage in the street. He did not let his rage turn into words. Then he sat down and said we would talk about it in the morning.","zh":"那封信来的时候，我爸脸上腾起一股冷怒，一时说不出话来。他这种怒气是安静的，比嚷嚷更吓人。他气坏了，却全憋在心里。他站在炉子边，看着锅里的热气往上冒。我胸口也能感到同样的情绪，还掺着一点害怕。失信的传闻在街上激起了真正的愤慨。他没让自己的怒火变成话。然后他坐下来，说这事明天早上再谈。"},"bug":{"tw":[["buggy",4],["tap",0],["defect",2],["worm",6],["tease",0],["insect",2],["cat",6],["intercept",0]],"en":"The new app had a small bug that no one could find at first. It ran in a buggy way when two people used it at once, and the defect showed only at night. My colleague said a hidden worm in the code might intercept the data from the tap of a finger. We had to sit and tease the problem apart line by line. A stray cat walked over the keyboard, and an insect flew into the lamp. By morning we had fixed it, and the whole thing ran smooth. Nobody on the team slept much that night. It had been a long night.","zh":"这个新应用有个小毛病，起初谁也没找出来。两个人同时用的时候它就卡卡的，那个缺陷只在夜里才冒头。我同事说，代码里可能藏着一条虫子似的东西，会截走指尖点一下传过去的数据。我们只得坐下来，一行一行地把问题拆开来看。一只野猫从键盘上走过去，一只小虫飞进了灯里。到早上我们把它修好了，整个东西跑得顺顺当当。那一夜队里没人睡多少。真是个长夜。"},"coast":{"tw":[["coaster",4],["slide",0],["skid",3],["view",2],["southern",6],["glide",0],["shore",2],["island",6]],"en":"We drove down the southern coast for two days, and the view never stopped changing. A small island sat far out on the water, and the road would glide along the shore for miles. Once the car began to slide on the wet stones, and the wheels would skid on every turn. My father had to slow down and hold the line. A coaster, a heavy ship, passed us far below on the sea. In the evening we stopped and put our cups on a paper mat, the kind they call a coaster, and watched the light fade. It was a good trip.","zh":"我们沿着南边的海岸开了两天，风景一路都在变。远处水面上卧着一座小岛，路一贴着海岸滑行就是好几里。有一次车轮在湿石头上打滑，每转一个弯都要趔趄一下。我爸只得放慢车速，把住方向。一条沿海的货船从我们下方远处的海面上驶过。傍晚我们停下，把杯子搁在一张纸垫上——就是那种叫杯垫的东西——看着天光慢慢暗下去。这趟走得不错。"},"compare":{"tw":[["comparative",4],["equate",0],["study",2],["translate",6],["liken",0],["inflect",2],["relate",6],["equivalence",0]],"en":"At the market my mother liked to compare the price of one basket with another. She would study each label and relate the weight to the cost. She said you could not equate a cheap thing with a good one, and that the equivalence was never exact. She would liken a good seller to an old friend, and she could translate a foreign label in seconds. A comparative look at two shops was, for her, a serious business. She even knew how the ending of a word could inflect its meaning. In the end she bought nothing that day. That was often how it went.","zh":"在市场上，我妈喜欢把一只篮子的价钱跟另一只作比较。她会研究每一张标签，把分量和价钱对上。她说便宜货不能跟好东西划等号，这种对等从来都不精确。她爱把一个好卖主比作老朋友，外文标签她几秒钟就能看明白。在她看来，把两家店横着一比，是件正经事。她甚至知道一个词的词尾怎么改变它的意思。最后那天她什么也没买。她常常就是这样。"},"current":{"tw":[["currency",4],["flow",2],["juice",3],["course",2],["term",6],["stream",0],["torrent",3],["due",6]],"en":"The old mill stood by the river, and the current there was stronger than anywhere else on the course. A steady flow of water turned the wheel, and in spring it became a real torrent. My uncle said the stream could carry a tree trunk in a minute. He had to pay a small sum in local currency to keep the right to use the water. The term of the lease was short, and the money was due in June. He drank a glass of juice and watched the river run. The current did not care about any of it.","zh":"那座老磨坊立在河边，那一段的水流比整条河别处都要急。一股稳稳的水推着水轮转，到了春天就成了一道真正的激流。我叔说，这条溪一分钟就能冲走一根树干。他得用当地的货币付一小笔钱，才能保留用水的权利。租约的期限很短，钱六月到期。他喝了一杯果汁，看着河水流。那股水流才不管这些。"},"accent":{"tw":[["stress",3],["drawl",3],["express",2],["slang",6],["emphasis",0],["underscore",3],["pronounce",2],["tongue",6],["emphasise",0]],"en":"My new neighbour speaks English with a soft accent I cannot place. She has a slow drawl that makes every word longer, and she will stress the last part of a sentence, as if to underscore it. In class she would use a little slang, and the teacher asked her to express the same idea in plain words. The emphasis was always on the meaning, never on the sound. She told me it is hard to pronounce some sounds with a tired tongue. Then she would emphasise the first letter and make us all laugh. I began to copy her accent without noticing.","zh":"我的新邻居说英语时带着一种我听不出是哪儿的口音。她说话慢悠悠的，把每个词都拖长，还总把句子的末了加重，像是在特别强调。上课时她会用几句俚语，老师就让她把同一个意思用大白话再讲一遍。她着重的永远是意思，从不是声音。她跟我说，舌头一累，有些音就发不好。接着她会把头一个字母咬得特别重，逗得我们全笑。我不知不觉就开始学她的口音了。"},"access":{"tw":[["approach",2],["address",3],["reach",2],["provide",6],["admission",0],["entrance",3],["right",2],["allow",6],["accession",4]],"en":"The old library had no proper access for people in wheelchairs, only a narrow entrance at the side. My aunt wrote to the council and asked them to provide a ramp. It took a year, and she had to address three different offices before anyone listened. She had the right to enter, she said, and so did everyone else. The manager agreed to allow the change, and the work began in spring. On the day it opened, the approach from the street was smooth and wide. Admission was free, as it always had been. A new accession of books arrived the same week, and the two events felt like one gift. Now anyone can reach the reading room alone.","zh":"那座老图书馆没有供坐轮椅的人进出的像样通道，只有侧面一个窄窄的入口。我姑姑给市议会写信，请他们修一道坡道。这事拖了一年，她跑了三个不同的部门才有人理。她说自己有进门的权利，别人也一样。负责人同意改，工程在春天动工。坡道开通那天，从街上过来的这一段又平又宽。同一周还新入藏了一批书，两件事凑在一起像是一份礼物。现在谁都能自己进到阅览室了。"},"advantage":{"tw":[["reward",0],["good",3],["benefit",2],["disadvantage",1],["gain",6],["vantage",0],["lead",3],["prefer",2],["chance",6]],"en":"Living near the market gave us one clear advantage: we never had to drive for food. My mother would say the real benefit was the time we saved, though my father saw it as a gain in money. From the top window she had a good vantage point and could watch the stalls open. There was a disadvantage too, because the noise began before six. I would prefer a quieter street, but I was outvoted. Sometimes a small reward came our way, a cheap box of fruit at the end of the day. It was pure chance, and it put us in the lead over our neighbours.","zh":"住在市场边上给了我们一个明显的好处：买东西从来不用开车。我妈说真正的好处是省下的时间，我爸却把它看成省下的钱。从顶楼的窗户望出去，位置很好，能看见摊位一个个开张。坏处也有，因为不到六点就吵起来了。我倒宁愿住条安静点的街，可投票我输了。有时还会有小便宜落到我们头上，一天收摊时买到一箱便宜水果。这纯属运气，却让我们在邻居面前占了先。"},"affect":{"tw":[["move",0],["hit",3],["change",2],["impact",6],["strike",0],["mouth",3],["refer",2],["risk",6],["touch",3]],"en":"The flood did not affect our street as badly as the one below the hill. Still, the impact was easy to see: the water had hit the low wall and left a mark. My father said a single storm could change a whole season, and it would move people to help one another. The wind seemed to strike the house from the side, and it left the ground soft underfoot. It did not touch our garden, though. When he spoke of the river, he would refer to it as the old enemy. We took no risk and stayed inside until the sky cleared. He wiped his mouth and went out to check the gate.","zh":"这场洪水对我们这条街的影响没山下那条街那么重。不过后果还是看得见：水撞上了矮墙，留下一道印子。我爸说一场暴风雨就能改变整个季节，也会让人动起来互相帮忙。风像是从侧面抽打这房子，把地面弄得踩上去软软的。还好没碰到我们的园子。他说起那条河的时候，总把它叫作老对头。我们没冒险，一直待在屋里等天放晴。他抹了抹嘴，出去查看大门。"},"aid":{"tw":[["care",3],["comfort",3],["better",2],["humanitarian",6],["help",0],["hand",3],["support",2],["provide",6],["assist",0]],"en":"After the storm, a small aid group came to our village with food and clean water. They did not just provide boxes; they gave real help and stayed to assist the old people in the lower houses. A nurse offered care to anyone who was hurt, and another woman brought comfort to those who had lost a roof. One of them gave me a hand with my grandmother, whose legs were no better than before. The group had no humanitarian name on their van, only a number. I asked if they were paid, and they laughed. It was the quietest kind of support I have ever seen.","zh":"暴风雨过后，一个小的援助团体带着食物和干净的水来到我们村。他们不只是发箱子，而是实实在在地帮忙，还留下来照顾住在低处那些房子里的老人。一位护士给所有受伤的人做护理，另一位妇女去安慰那些屋顶被掀掉的人。有一个人帮我一起照看我奶奶，她的腿还是不见好转。那辆车上没有印任何慈善机构的名字，只有一个编号。我问他们有没有报酬，他们笑了。这是我这辈子见过最安静的支援。"},"alarm":{"tw":[["alert",0],["wake",3],["clock",2],["panic",6],["appal",0],["shock",3],["fear",2],["trigger",6],["dismay",0]],"en":"The alarm went off at two in the morning, and the whole building was awake in seconds. My father was the first to wake; he had always been a light sleeper and quick to alert. The old clock by the door showed the time, and its face seemed to glow. For a moment I felt pure panic, then a cold fear that would not leave. What had triggered it, nobody knew. The noise seemed to appal even the cat. We stood outside in the street, and the shock turned slowly into dismay when we saw smoke at the far end. It was only a kitchen fire, but none of us slept again.","zh":"警报在凌晨两点响了，整栋楼几秒钟内全醒了。我爸最先醒来，他一向睡得浅，反应也快。门边那只旧钟指着时间，钟面像是在发光。有那么一瞬间我只觉得惊恐，接着是一股赶不走的、冰凉的恐惧。是什么触发的，谁也不知道。那声音连猫都吓住了。我们站到街上，等看见那头冒烟时，惊吓慢慢变成了沮丧。不过是个厨房着火，可我们再没睡着。"},"announce":{"tw":[["declare",0],["advertise",3],["inform",2],["postpone",6],["herald",0],["cry",3],["state",2],["cancel",6],["denote",0]],"en":"The station manager came out to announce that the evening train would not run. He had to declare it twice, because the wind took his voice away. A small notice on the board would inform the rest of the passengers, he said, and he promised to advertise the change in the local paper. They had to postpone the whole journey until Monday, and the office would cancel every ticket. A cry went up from the waiting room, half anger and half relief. He would state the reason once more. Nobody wanted to be the herald of such news. The clock above the door seemed to denote only delay. I sat down again with my bag and waited.","zh":"站长出来宣布晚上的那趟车不开了。他不得不讲了两遍，风把他的嗓子都吹散了。他说墙上那张小告示会通知其余乘客，还答应在本地报纸上登出来。整趟行程只好推迟到周一，售票处会把所有票都退掉。候车室里响起一片喊声，一半是气，一半是松口气。谁都不愿当这种消息的报信人。门上方那只钟仿佛只表示一件事：晚点。我拎着包又坐了下来，继续等。"},"approach":{"tw":[["near",0],["chip",3],["act",2],["practical",6],["access",3],["close",3],["address",2],["view",6],["advance",0]],"en":"The door would not close, and my grandfather had a simple approach to fixing it. He would not act in a hurry; he liked to look first and then advance slowly. A small chip near the hinge told him the wood had moved. The house stood near the sea, he said, and salt does that to everything. His view was practical: find the cause, then address it. I held the lamp while he worked, and from this close I could see every line in his hands. He never needed extra access to the back room, and he finished before dark. The door closed quietly for the first time in years.","zh":"那扇门关不上了，我爷爷修它有他自己简单的一套办法。他从不着急动手，喜欢先看清楚，再一点点往前推进。铰链旁崩了一小块，让他看出木头错位了。他说这房子靠海，盐分对什么东西都这样。他的看法很实在：先找出原因，再对症下手。他干活时我举着灯，凑得这么近，我把他手上的每一道纹路都看清了。他根本不用进里屋拿东西，天黑前就修好了。那扇门多年来头一回关得没声。"},"assist":{"tw":[["help",2],["care",3],["support",2],["manage",6],["serve",0],["comfort",3],["work",2],["improve",6],["aid",0]],"en":"At the front desk a clerk will assist anyone who looks lost, and my aunt did that job for years. She would serve the older customers first and take care not to rush them. Her real work was to manage small problems before they grew. When a family came in with a sick child, she would offer comfort as much as support. She liked to improve the little things: a chair by the door, a clear sign, a lamp that was not too bright. Once she gave a man the last of her own aid money for a bus. She said it helped her more than it helped him.","zh":"前台有个职员会去帮任何看着迷路的人，这活儿我姑干了多年。她总先招呼上了年纪的顾客，注意不催他们。她真正的工作是在小麻烦变大之前把它处理好。要是有家人带着生病的孩子进来，她给的既是帮助也是安慰。她喜欢把那些小地方弄好一点：门边一把椅子、一块清楚的牌子、一盏不太亮的灯。有一回她还把自己身上最后一点救济钱给了一个人坐车。她说这钱帮了她自己更多，倒不是帮了那人。"},"attempt":{"tw":[["try",0],["best",3],["act",2],["escape",6],["attack",0],["fight",3],["initiate",2],["step",6],["seek",0]],"en":"Their first attempt to reach the climber failed in the dark, and the second one began at dawn. The rescue team would try again with better ropes, and every member did his best. Nobody wanted to act alone on that face of the rock. There was a real risk of an attack from falling stone, and the cold could fight them all the way. They had to initiate the climb from a lower step, then seek a safer line to the left. It was not an escape from danger but a slow walk into it. When they finally came down with the man alive, we could not speak.","zh":"他们第一次想够到那个攀岩者的努力在天黑时失败了，第二回天一亮就开始了。救援队换了更好的绳子再试，每个人都尽了全力。在那面岩壁上，谁都不想单独行动。落石砸下来的风险是实打实的，寒气也一路跟他们作对。他们得从低一级的地方起步，再往左边找一条更安全的线路。这不是逃开危险，而是一步步走进危险里去。等他们终于带着那个活着的人下来时，我们一句话都说不出来。"},"avoid":{"tw":[["avert",0],["miss",3],["prevent",2],["confront",1],["stop",6],["annul",0],["hedge",3],["cancel",2],["quash",0]],"en":"My uncle tried to avoid a bad deal with a man he did not trust. He wanted to prevent any loss before it began, so he asked a lawyer to check every line. The lawyer said he could annul the whole agreement if the figures were wrong, and a judge might quash it later. To avert trouble, my uncle walked away and let the offer die. He would not confront the man face to face. He even asked a friend to cancel the meeting for him. He simply missed it on purpose and stopped answering his phone. I once saw him hide behind a hedge to avoid the same man in the street.","zh":"我叔想躲开一笔跟一个他不信的人的烂交易。他想在损失发生前就防住，于是请律师把每一条都查了一遍。律师说，只要数字有问题，他就能把整份协议作废，法官日后还可能把它推翻。为了避开麻烦，我叔干脆走人，让那笔生意黄掉。他不愿跟那人当面碰头，就故意不去开会，还不接电话。有一回我在街上看见他躲在一道树篱后面，就为了躲开同一个人。"},"band":{"tw":[["lot",0],["party",3],["tie",2],["rock",6],["ring",0],["weed",3],["attach",2],["punk",6],["circle",0]],"en":"A small band played at the village party last summer, and a lot of people came. They had one tie between them: all four had gone to the same school and loved rock. There was a thin ring on the singer's finger, and he wore an old punk shirt. They would attach a long cable to the lamp post for power. My job was to weed the square before they set up, which took all morning. Children danced in a circle near the front, and an old couple held hands in the middle of the road. When the music stopped, nobody wanted to go home, and the band played one more song.","zh":"去年夏天村里聚会上来了一支小乐队，来了好多人。他们之间有一条纽带：四个人上的是同一所学校，都喜欢摇滚。主唱手指上戴着一枚细戒指，穿着一件旧朋克T恤。他们要把一根长电缆接到路灯柱上取电。我的活儿是在他们搭台之前把广场上的草拔干净，一拔就是一上午。孩子们在前头围成一圈跳舞，一对老夫妻在路当中牵着手。音乐一停，谁都不想回家，乐队又加唱了一首。"},"bare":{"tw":[["air",3],["circulate",3],["clear",2],["thick",6],["simple",0],["publish",3],["tell",2],["beneath",6],["plain",5]],"en":"The room was almost bare when we moved in: four walls, one window, and nothing else. The air did not circulate, and on a thick summer day it was hard to breathe. My mother said a simple house should stay simple, and she would not publish her plans to anyone outside the family. She liked to tell us the truth about money. Beneath the bare floorboards there was old paper, and when we cleared it out we found a name. The light was plain and white, and it made the empty space look honest. I slept better there than in any rich house.","zh":"搬进来时那屋子几乎是空的：四面墙、一扇窗，别的什么都没有。空气不流通，闷热的夏天里连喘气都费劲。我妈说简单的房子就该保持简单，她也不会把自家的打算说给外人听。她喜欢跟我们说实话，尤其是钱的事。光秃秃的地板下面垫着旧报纸，我们清出来的时候发现了一个名字。光是素白的，照得这片空地方看着很实在。我在那儿睡得比在任何阔气的房子里都好。"},"battle":{"tw":[["fight",0],["war",3],["attempt",2],["siege",6],["struggle",0],["assault",3],["contend",2],["skirmish",6],["combat",3]],"en":"My aunt's long battle with her illness lasted almost four years. She would fight every morning to get out of bed, and each small step was an attempt at something larger. There was no war, no open assault on her body, only a quiet struggle that wore her down. In the last winter it felt like a siege, with the cold on one side and the pain on the other. She and the doctors would contend over every treatment, and sometimes it was a skirmish about a single pill. Yet she never gave up the combat. She said the body is a field, and you do not leave it to the weeds.","zh":"我姑跟病魔的那场长期斗争拖了将近四年。每天早上她都要挣扎着才起得来，每一小步都是想做成更大一件事的尝试。没有战争，也没有对她身体发起的猛攻，只有一种慢慢把人拖垮的无声较量。最后那个冬天，像是一场围困，一边是寒冷，一边是疼痛。她和医生为每一种疗法争执，有时只为一片药就起一场小冲突。可她从没放弃过这场搏斗。她说身体就是一块地，你不能让它荒着长草。"},"bend":{"tw":[["bow",0],["lean",3],["move",2],["fork",6],["fold",0],["arch",3],["ordinary",2],["slope",6],["twist",0]],"en":"The road began to bend just past the old mill, where a narrow fork led down to the water. My father would lean on the gate and watch the cars slow there. In winter the slope could move with the rain, and the whole edge would fall away. He used to bow his head against the wind and walk on. Above the door a small arch had been cut into the stone, an ordinary detail nobody noticed. He showed me how to fold a paper map so it would not tear, and how to twist the wire to hold a fence. I still take that bend slowly, out of respect for him.","zh":"过了那座旧磨坊，路就开始弯了，那里有条窄窄的岔路通向水边。我爸总爱趴在门上，看着车子在那儿慢下来。冬天雨水一泡，这个斜坡会松动，整条路边都能塌下去。他从前都是低着头顶着风往前走。门上方石头上凿着一个小小的拱，是个没人会留意的寻常细节。他教我怎么把纸地图折好才不撕，还教我怎么拧铁丝才能固定住篱笆。到现在我过那个弯还开得很慢，算是敬他。"},"bind":{"tw":[["hold",0],["article",3],["cover",2],["inhibit",6],["stick",0],["band",3],["relate",2],["interact",6],["tie",0]],"en":"The old man would bind books by hand in a room above the shop. He used a thin band of cloth to hold the pages, and a strip of glue to stick the cover down. Every article he repaired came back looking new. He said a good binding should relate to how the book is used, not to fashion. He would tie the thread twice and then press it flat. Strong glue could inhibit him from opening the book later, so he used little. I liked to watch the paper interact with the cloth under his hands. It was slow work, and he never hurried.","zh":"那位老人会在店楼上的屋子里手工装订书。他用一条细细的布带把书页固定住，再用一条胶把封面粘牢。他修好的每一篇东西拿回来都像新的。他说好的装订要跟书怎么用有关系，而不是赶时髦。他总把线打两个结，再压平。胶太强以后就翻不开书，所以他用得很少。我爱看他手底下纸张和布怎么互相贴合。这活儿很慢，他从不着急。"},"bite":{"tw":[["bit",0],["chew",3],["meal",2],["cat",6],["burn",0],["snap",3],["taste",2],["mouth",6],["sting",0]],"en":"That first bite told me the soup was far too hot. I had to chew slowly and let the taste settle before I took another. My grandmother watched from her chair with the cat on her lap, and she said a good meal should never burn your mouth. A fly came in and tried to sting my hand, and I nearly let the spoon snap against the bowl. She told me to take a bit of bread to cool it. There was a small sting of shame too, because I had said the food was ready. I ate the rest in silence and said nothing.","zh":"第一口就告诉我这汤太烫了。我只得慢慢嚼，让味道落定，再动第二口。我奶奶坐在椅子上看着，猫趴在她腿上，她说一顿好饭绝不该烫嘴。一只苍蝇飞进来想蜇我的手，我差点让勺子磕到碗上。她让我拿一小块面包降降温。还有一点羞愧像针一样扎着我，因为是我说饭好了的。剩下的我一声不响地吃完，什么也没说。"},"block":{"tw":[["bar",0],["cake",3],["hide",2],["main",6],["forget",0],["check",3],["hold",2],["drive",6],["freeze",0]],"en":"One block of ice had frozen solid on the step outside, and nobody could move it. My father had to drive into town for salt and a metal bar. He would forget the cold once he was working, and he did not check the weather first. I tried to hold the door open for him, but the wind kept pulling it shut. The main road was white, and he had to hide the car behind the wall so it would not slide. Later my mother brought out a piece of cake and we ate it watching the ice melt. It was gone by evening.","zh":"外面台阶上结着一大块冰，冻得死死的，谁也挪不动。我爸只好开车进城买盐和一根铁撬棍。他一干起活来就忘了冷，出门前也没看天气。我想给他把门扶住，可风老把门往回抽。大路上全是白的，他只好把车藏到墙后面，免得滑出去。后来我妈端出一块蛋糕，我们边吃边看那块冰化。到傍晚它就没了。"},"boil":{"tw":[["churn",0],["simmer",3],["change",2],["freeze",1],["stir",6],["seethe",3],["ferment",3],["move",2],["soak",6]],"en":"My grandmother would let the soup boil for an hour, then turn it down to simmer. She liked to stir it with a long wooden spoon and watch the surface churn. The whole kitchen would soak in steam, and the windows would go white. She said a good cook must let the flavours change slowly, not all at once. When the pot began to seethe, she would move it off the heat and wait. In winter she would freeze what was left. Once she tried to ferment cabbage in the same pot, and the smell filled the house for days.","zh":"我奶奶会让汤滚上一个钟头，再转小火慢慢煨。她喜欢拿长木勺搅，看表面翻腾打转。整间厨房都浸在蒸汽里，窗户蒙上一层白。她说好厨子得让味道慢慢变，不能一下子全出来。锅一开始咕嘟翻涌，她就把火撤了等着。冬天她会把剩下的冻起来。有一回她想用同一口锅腌酸菜，那味儿在家里熏了好几天。"},"bond":{"tw":[["stick",0],["fixate",3],["relate",2],["interest",6],["tie",0],["handcuff",3],["unite",2],["price",6],["attach",2]],"en":"The bond between my father and his old partner began in a small repair shop. They would stick labels on boxes together and tie parcels with the same rough string. It was hard to relate their two lives, yet the work made them unite in a quiet way. My father had once been a guard and still kept a pair of handcuffs in a drawer, a joke between them. The partner would fixate on one small fault. Every price in the shop was written by the partner, and every repair was done by my father. He would attach a note to each finished job. I saw their interest in each other was real.","zh":"我爸和他老搭档之间的那份情谊，是从一家小修理铺开始的。他们一起给箱子贴标签，用同一根粗绳捆包裹。要把他们俩截然不同的日子说到一块儿很难，可这活儿让他们悄悄结成了一体。我爸以前当过看守，抽屉里还留着一副手铐，这是两人之间的一个玩笑。搭档会盯着一个小毛病抠半天。铺子里每个价钱都是搭档写的，每件活儿都是我爸修的。他会在修好的东西上贴一张便条。我看出他对对方的关心是真心的。"},"bow":{"tw":[["bend",2],["scrape",3],["front",2],["pin",6],["stem",0],["rainbow",3],["play",2],["nose",6],["submit",0]],"en":"My sister made a small bow from a bent stem of ash and a length of string. She would bend it until it almost broke and then let it spring. The front of the target was worn, so she would pin a paper circle over it. Her arrows would scrape the wall behind and fall into the wet grass. She liked to play at being a hunter, and she never missed a rainbow in the sky after the rain. One day she hit the centre and had to submit to a loud cheer. She wiped her nose with her sleeve and smiled.","zh":"我妹妹用一根弯的白蜡树条和一段绳子做了一把小弓。她能把弓压得快要断了，再让它弹回去。靶子的正面磨旧了，她就在上面钉一个纸圆圈。她的箭会擦到后面的墙，掉进湿草里。她喜欢扮猎人玩，雨后天上要是有彩虹，她一次都没漏看。有一天她射中了正中，只好接受大家的一阵叫好。她用袖子抹了抹鼻子，笑了。"},"brush":{"tw":[["sweep",0],["brake",3],["clean",2],["thick",6],["encounter",0],["flick",3],["cover",2],["mud",6],["clash",0]],"en":"After the long walk my boots were covered in thick mud, so I took a stiff brush to them by the door. I had to sweep the floor twice, because the dirt went everywhere. My father would flick the loose pieces into the yard and then clean the bristles with an old cloth. At the corner of the lane we had had a small encounter with a dog, which ran off before we could brake. There was a clash of colour on the brush where the red mud met the grey. It took an hour before the shoes were dry enough to cover with polish.","zh":"走了那么远，我的靴子上糊着厚厚的泥，于是我在门口拿硬毛刷子刷。地得扫两遍，因为泥渣弄得到处都是。我爸会把松散的那点渣弹到院子里，再用旧布把刷毛擦干净。在小巷拐角，我们还跟一条狗小遇了一场，它没等我们刹住就跑开了。刷子上红泥和灰泥碰在一块儿，颜色冲得厉害。擦鞋得等上一个钟头，鞋子干得能上油才行。"},"buck":{"tw":[["charge",0],["rip",3],["bill",2],["chuck",6],["horse",2],["stag",3],["move",2],["wolf",6],["shoot",0]],"en":"A ride on the old horse cost ten bucks, and my brother did not have enough. He asked the man to charge him less, but the man just laughed and said no. My brother would rip the ticket in his hand and chuck it in the bin. Near the gate a stone stag stood in the grass, and someone had painted a wolf beside it. He watched a girl shoot at a target for a while, then move away without a word. My father paid the bill without saying anything either. On the way home my brother said he would save his own money next time.","zh":"骑一次那匹老马要十块钱，我弟弟钱不够。他求那人少收点，那人只笑笑说不行。我弟就把手里的票撕了，扔进垃圾桶。大门附近的草里立着一头石头雄鹿，有人在旁边画了只狼。他看一个女孩对着靶子射了一会儿，然后一句话不说走开了。我爸也一声不响把账付了。回家路上，我弟说下次要自己攒钱。"},"bump":{"tw":[["blow",0],["throw",3],["dance",2],["bounce",6],["break",0],["reduce",3],["hit",2],["swing",6],["chance",0]],"en":"There was a small bump on the baby's head after she fell from the low swing. My mother saw the whole thing: one hard blow, a short cry, and then silence. She would not let us throw the swing away, though; she just made the seat lower. The little one began to dance again within minutes, which told us it was nothing. A doctor said we could reduce the risk by moving the swing nearer the grass. Every bounce of the old rope seemed to worry my father, and he would hit the frame with his hand to test it. It was pure chance she was not hurt when the branch broke.","zh":"那孩子从矮秋千上摔下来，头上起了个小包。我妈全看见了：一下重重的撞击，一声短哭，然后就没声了。可她不让我们把秋千扔了，只把座板放低了些。小家伙几分钟就又跳起舞来，我们便知道没事。一位医生说，把秋千挪近草地就能减少风险。旧绳子每弹一下，我爸都好像悬着心，还会用手敲敲架子试结实不结实。树枝断的时候她没伤着，纯粹是运气。"},"burst":{"tw":[["break",2],["blow",3],["fire",2],["tear",6],["fit",0],["pop",3],["jump",2],["wave",6],["split",0]],"en":"An old pipe under the kitchen floor began to split, and then it burst with a loud pop. The water came through in a wave that covered the whole room in minutes. My father heard the break from the garden and ran in, and I saw him jump back from the cold. He had to fit a new piece before the fire could be lit again. There was a short blow of anger when he saw the mess, and he nearly let a tear fall. I helped him dry the floor with every cloth we had. He said a house is always telling you something, and we should listen.","zh":"厨房地板下面一根旧管子先裂了缝，接着砰的一声爆开。水成片涌出来，几分钟就把整间屋子淹了。我爸在园子里听见爆裂声跑进来，我看见他被冰水激得往后一跳。他得换上一截新的，炉子才能再生火。看见这满地狼藉，他发了一小通火，差点儿掉下泪来。我拿家里所有的布帮他擦地。他说房子总在跟你说话，你得听着。"},"channel":{"tw":[["carry",0],["send",3],["move",2],["network",6],["line",0],["track",3],["passage",2],["broadcast",6],["transfer",0]],"en":"The local channel had one small studio and a tower on the hill. They would carry the morning news and send it out to every village in the valley. A thin line of cable ran along the old railway track to the next town. From there the signal had to move through a wider network that nobody could see. The passage between the hills, they said, was the reason we got any broadcast at all. Every evening a woman read the weather, and then the screen would transfer to a film. I watched the same channel for years, and it felt like a friend.","zh":"本地那个频道只有一间小演播室和山上的一座发射塔。他们把早间新闻接过来，再发往山谷里每个村子。一根细细的缆线沿着旧铁轨一直通到下一个镇子。信号从那儿还得穿过一张谁也看不见的更大的网。他们说，正是两山之间那道通道让我们能收到信号。每天傍晚有位女士读天气预报，接着屏幕就转到电影。同一个频道我看了好些年，感觉像个老朋友。"},"charm":{"tw":[["catch",0],["hold",3],["control",2],["wit",6],["appeal",0],["work",3],["attract",2],["charisma",6],["influence",0]],"en":"The old shopkeeper had a charm that no one could explain. He would catch your eye and nod. He did not try to control anyone, yet people would stop and listen to him. His wit was quick, and his stories had a strange appeal that seemed to work on everyone. He could attract a crowd with a single word, and he would hold their attention while he weighed the fruit. I used to think it was a kind of charisma, a gift you are born with. Later I saw it was only care: he remembered names, and he asked about your children. That quiet influence was stronger than any trick of speech.","zh":"那位老店主身上有种谁也说不上来的魅力。他会迎上你的目光，点点头。他从不试图管着谁，可人们都愿意停下来听他说话。他机灵风趣，他的故事有一种奇特的吸引力，好像对谁都管用。他一句话就能招来一圈人，边称水果边把大家的注意力攥住。我原以为这是一种天生的领袖魅力。后来我才看明白，那不过是用心：他记得住名字，还会问起你的孩子。那种不声不响的影响，比任何说话技巧都强。"},"chase":{"tw":[["dog",0],["tree",3],["cut",2],["tail",0],["quest",3],["frame",2],["street",6],["track",0]],"en":"Our dog would chase his own tail in the yard until he fell over. One afternoon he ran out through a gap in the fence and down the street. My brother cut across the field to head him off, following the track beside the old tree. It was a small quest, but he took it seriously and would not give up. I stayed by the gate and watched the frame of the doorway, waiting. When the dog came back, muddy and happy, he jumped into my arms. He had no idea it was only a game. Some evenings I still see them running in my mind.","zh":"我家那条狗会在院子里追自己的尾巴，一直追到自己摔倒。一天下午它从篱笆的缺口跑出去，顺着街一路跑。我弟抄近路穿过田地拦它，沿着老树旁的那条小径追。算不上什么大任务，可他当成正经事，绝不肯罢手。我守着大门，盯着门框那边等着。狗回来时浑身是泥，高兴得直蹦，一下扑进我怀里。它根本不知道那只是闹着玩。有些傍晚，我还能在脑子里看见他们俩跑。"},"cheat":{"tw":[["screw",0],["job",3],["beat",2],["dare",6],["wander",0],["shark",3],["fraud",2],["shaft",0]],"en":"One player tried to cheat at the card game, and my uncle caught him at once. He had a screw loose, my uncle said, to dare such a thing in a room full of people. The man tried to beat the table with his hand and shout that it was fraud. Nobody believed him; his whole job was selling cheap goods that fell apart. He began to wander between the tables, looking for someone to blame. Then he grabbed his coat and went out, past the fish stall with its shark, down to the dark shaft of the old lift. We finished the game without him.","zh":"有个牌局上的人想作弊，我叔一眼就逮住了他。我叔说，敢在一屋子人面前干这种事，他脑子里少根弦。那人拿手拍桌子，嚷嚷说是骗局。没人信他；他这辈子的活儿就是卖那些用两下就散架的便宜货。他开始在桌间转来转去，想找个人赖。然后他抓起外套出去了，经过那个摆着鲨鱼的鱼摊，走到旧电梯黑洞洞的竖井边。我们没等他就把牌打完了。"},"cheer":{"tw":[["inspire",0],["amuse",3],["attribute",2],["complain",1],["crowd",6],["urge",0],["bravo",3],["applaud",2],["delight",6]],"en":"When the young singer finished, the whole crowd rose to cheer. People began to applaud, and someone near the door shouted bravo. It was hard to attribute the joy in the room to any one thing: the song, the light, or the fact that we had all waited so long. She seemed to inspire the old as much as amuse the young. My mother, who would complain about any noise at home, was the first to urge her to sing again. I felt a quiet delight I had not expected. For once nobody wanted the evening to end.","zh":"那位年轻歌手唱完，满场的人都站起来欢呼。大家开始鼓掌，门边有人喊了一声“好”。屋里这股高兴劲儿到底是因为什么，很难说得清：是那首歌，是灯光，还是我们等得太久了。她好像既能给老人鼓劲，又能逗乐年轻人。我妈在家里有点动静都要抱怨，这回却头一个催她再唱一首。我心里有一种没料到的、静静的欢喜。这一回谁都不想散场。"},"clip":{"tw":[["dress",0],["top",3],["blow",2],["video",6],["crop",0],["poll",3],["cut",2],["tape",6],["trim",0]],"en":"The farmer took a short video of his wheat before the harvest, then used a clip of it in a small local poll about the weather. He had learned to trim the crop by hand when the machines could not come up the hill. On the tape you can hear the wind give one long blow across the field. He wore a plain dress shirt to the meeting, with the top button open, because he wanted to look serious. A woman with a cut on her hand stood next to him and said the season had been hard. He nodded and said nothing more.","zh":"收麦子前那位农人给自家麦田拍了一小段视频，后来把其中一小段用在了当地一个关于天气的小调查里。机器上不了山的时候，他学会了用手修剪麦子。在那盘带子里能听见风从田上长长地刮过去。去开会时他穿了一件素色衬衫，最上面的扣子敞着，因为他想显得郑重些。一位手上带着伤口的女人站在他旁边，说这一年不好过。他点点头，没再多说。"},"cloud":{"tw":[["corrupt",0],["mushroom",3],["change",2],["dust",6],["fog",0],["haze",3],["hide",2],["beneath",6],["mist",0]],"en":"By morning a low cloud had come down over the valley, and the mist made the far fields disappear. My father said the weather would change before noon; the haze on the hill was always a sign. In the fog we could hardly find the road, and the dust from the dry ground hung in the air. A single mushroom grew beneath the old oak, and he would hide it in his basket so that nobody else would find it. The smell from the ditch was corrupt, he said, and it worried him. By evening the whole sky had cleared, and the moon rose over the empty fields.","zh":"到早上，一片低云压到了山谷上，雾气把远处的田都吞了。我爸说中午前天气会变，山上那层霭从来都是个兆头。雾里我们几乎找不着路，干地上扬起的尘土悬在半空。老橡树底下长了一朵蘑菇，他会把它藏进篮子，不让别人找到。他说沟渠里那股味儿已经坏了，这让他不安。到了傍晚天全放晴了，月亮从空荡荡的田上升起来。"},"combine":{"tw":[["trust",0],["melt",3],["add",2],["mixture",6],["mix",0],["absorb",3],["contribute",2],["butter",6],["unite",0]],"en":"To make the cake she would combine the flour and the butter with her hands. First she would mix them until they looked like sand, then add a little sugar and let it absorb the water. The whole mixture had to rest before it went into the oven. She said the heat would melt the butter and unite everything inside. I liked to contribute by beating the eggs, though I always made a mess. There was a trust between us in that kitchen that I have never found anywhere else. We never wrote the recipe down. Years later I still make it the same way, badly.","zh":"做那块蛋糕时，她会用手把面粉和黄油揉到一块儿。先拌成沙子那样的粗细，再加一点糖，让它把水吸进去。整盆面糊得先歇一会儿才能进烤箱。她说温度会把黄油化开，让里面的东西合成一体。我喜欢帮着打鸡蛋，虽然每次都弄得一团糟。在那个厨房里，我们之间有一种信任，我后来在别处再没找到过。配方我们从来没写下来。多年以后我还是照老样子做，做得还是不行。"},"command":{"tw":[["control",0],["call",3],["lie",2],["corps",6],["require",0],["charge",3],["order",2],["force",6],["bid",0]],"en":"The fire chief stood in the yard and gave one clear command to the whole corps. Nobody had to call twice; everyone knew what was required of them. He would not use force or shout, though he had the power to control every movement. His order was simple: get the hose up the hill before the wind turned. Later there was a bid for new equipment, and he had to charge a young officer with the paperwork. He would lie awake that night, going over every step again. I learned more from him in one week than in a year of school.","zh":"消防队长站在院子里，向整个队下了一道清清楚楚的命令。没人需要喊第二遍，大家都清楚自己该做什么。他不用强逼，也不大声吼，尽管他有本事掌控每一个动作。他的命令很简单：趁风没转向，把水带拖上山。后来有个采购新装备的申请，他得把文书的事交给一个年轻队员。那晚他躺在床上翻来覆去，把每一步又过了一遍。跟他干一个星期，我学到的比在学校一年还多。"},"concentrate":{"tw":[["centre",0],["listen",3],["change",2],["continue",6],["contract",0],["think",3],["decrease",2],["develop",6],["reduce",0]],"en":"It was hard to concentrate in the room because the builders would not stop working outside. I tried to listen to my own breathing, but the noise would change every few minutes. The centre of the table was covered with books I had to read before the exam. I could not think, so I decided to continue in the garden instead. There the traffic seemed to decrease and the air was cooler. My father said that when a plan does not work, you reduce it to the smallest step and develop from there. The college had a contract with the library, so I could study late. I passed the exam in the end.","zh":"在屋里很难专心，因为外头施工的人不停。我想听自己的呼吸，可噪声每几分钟就变一次。桌子正中间堆满了我考试前要看的书。我根本想不下去，索性决定挪到园子里接着看。那边车声好像少了，空气也凉些。我爸说过，一个办法行不通时，就把它缩成最小的一步，从那儿再往下做。学院跟图书馆有协议，所以我可以在那儿学到很晚。最后我考过了。"},"condition":{"tw":[["check",0],["need",3],["better",2],["patient",6],["shape",0],["ordinary",3],["state",2],["failure",6],["term",0]],"en":"Before I bought the old car, a mechanic came to check its condition. He said the engine was in better shape than he had expected, but the back door would need work. The owner, a quiet man, waited like a patient in a waiting room while we talked. It was an ordinary car, nothing special, and that was why I liked it. He told me the real state of the tyres and did not hide the small failure in the lights. On our terms, the price came down a little. I drove it home that evening with the window open.","zh":"买那辆旧车前，一位修车师傅来检查它的状况。他说发动机比他想的好，不过后车门得修。车主是个安静的人，我们说话时他像个候诊的病人似的等着。那是辆很普通的车，没什么特别的，我恰恰因此喜欢它。他如实讲了轮胎的真实情况，也没瞒着车灯那个小毛病。按我们谈好的条件，价钱降了一点。那天傍晚我开着它回家，一路开着窗。"},"contact":{"tw":[["meet",0],["cover",3],["channel",2],["telephone",6],["reach",0],["raise",3],["communicate",2],["without",6],["touch",0]],"en":"I lost contact with my old classmate after we left school, and I never knew how to reach her. Then one evening the telephone rang and it was her. We had to meet somewhere quiet, so I chose the small cafe by the river. She looked well, and we talked for hours, without needing to cover anything up. I asked how she had learned to communicate with so many people in her new job, and she laughed. She had worked her way up and had even helped raise money for a channel that teaches children. When we parted, she touched my arm and said we should not wait another ten years.","zh":"离开学校以后，我就跟那位老同学断了联系，一直不知道该怎么找到她。后来一天傍晚电话响了，是她。我们得找个安静的地方见面，我就挑了河边那家小咖啡馆。她气色不错，我们聊了好几个钟头，什么都不用遮着瞒着。我问她是怎么学会在新工作里跟那么多人打交道的，她笑了。她是一点点熬上来的，还帮着为一个教孩子的频道筹过钱。分别时她碰了碰我的胳膊，说别再等上十年才见。"},"cope":{"tw":[["deal",0],["meet",3],["act",2],["relieve",6],["manage",0],["hack",3],["brick",2],["suffer",6],["contend",0]],"en":"My uncle had to cope with a hard year after the shop closed. He would deal with one problem at a time and act as if nothing were wrong. He had a bad hack in his chest from the dust, and he could not manage the long walk up the hill. His wife would meet him at the gate and try to relieve his worry with a hot meal. He had to contend with the bank, the landlord, and his own pride. Sometimes he would suffer in silence rather than ask for help. In the end he sold the last brick of the old wall to pay them off.","zh":"店关门以后，我叔得撑过艰难的一年。他总是一件一件地解决问题，还装作什么都没发生。他胸口让灰尘呛出了久咳，走那段上坡路也撑不住。他妻子会在门口迎他，想用一顿热饭给他解解愁。他得跟银行、房东还有自己的脸面较劲。有时他宁可闷着受着，也不肯开口求人。最后他把老墙上最后一块砖也卖了，才把账还清。"},"copy":{"tw":[["imitate",0],["follow",3],["write",2],["read",6],["transcript",0],["cast",3],["material",2],["text",6],["replicate",0]],"en":"The teacher asked us to copy the whole text by hand, so that we would remember it. I would write one line, then read it back, and then write it again. My friend could imitate the teacher's voice perfectly, and he would make the class laugh. We were told not to follow the old style too closely, but to make our own transcript of the story. The material we used was cheap paper, and the cast of characters was small. I tried to replicate the ending in my own words, and the teacher said it was better than the original. I still have that copy somewhere.","zh":"老师让我们把整篇课文手抄一遍，好记住它。我抄一行，念回来，再抄一遍。我朋友能把老师的嗓音模仿得一模一样，逗得全班大笑。老师叫我们别把老风格跟得太死，但要做出自己的一份故事记录。我们用的纸很便宜，出场的人物也不多。我试着自己重写那个结尾，老师说比原来的还好。那份抄本我到现在还留着，就在某个地方。"},"correct":{"tw":[["right",0],["carry",3],["change",2],["precise",6],["discipline",0],["cover",3],["treat",2],["compare",6],["adjust",0]],"en":"The shop gave me the correct change for my note, and I counted it twice. My mother would always check that the numbers were precise before we left the counter. She had a quiet discipline about money that I did not understand then. At home we would carry the receipt to the table and compare it with her own small book. She would cover the total with one hand. If something was wrong, she would go back, not to argue, but to adjust the record. She would treat every shop the same way. It took me years to see that she was simply being right.","zh":"店里找给我的钱数目没错，我数了两遍。我妈总要在离开柜台前把数字核对准。她对钱有一种安静的规矩，我当时并不懂。回到家，我们会把收据拿到桌上，跟她自己那个小本子对一遍。她会用一只手把总数盖住。要是哪儿不对，她就回去，不是去吵，而是去改记录。她对哪家店都一个态度。过了好些年我才明白，她不过就是坚持把事情弄对。"},"crash":{"tw":[["smash",0],["wrap",3],["change",2],["collision",6],["collapse",0],["ditch",3],["hit",2],["plane",6],["clash",0]],"en":"The crash happened on the bend after the bridge, where the road narrows between two walls. A small van had tried to pass a lorry and could not stop in time. The collision left one wheel in the ditch and glass across the road. My father heard the smash and ran out; he said it was a wonder nobody was hit. The clash of metal seemed to change the quiet of the whole street in a second. He would wrap a coat round a shaking driver and wait for help. For years after, whenever a plane passed low overhead, he would stop and look up, as if the sky might collapse.","zh":"那场撞车发生在过桥后的那个弯道上，路面在两道墙之间变窄。一辆小货车想超一辆卡车，没来得及刹住。碰撞之后，一个轮子陷进了沟里，玻璃撒了一地。我爸听见那声巨响跑出去，他说居然没人被撞到，真是万幸。金属相撞的刺耳声一瞬间就改变了整条街的安静。他会用外套裹住那个发抖的司机，等着人来救。此后好些年，只要飞机低低地掠过，他都会停下来抬头看，好像天会垮下来似的。"},"crowd":{"tw":[["push",0],["mass",3],["meet",2],["cheer",6],["gang",0],["pour",3],["move",2],["throng",6],["herd",0]],"en":"A large crowd had gathered outside the station, and more people seemed to pour in from every street. I had to push my way to the front to find my sister. A whole mass of travellers waited for news of the delayed train. Then someone began to cheer, and the sound moved through the crowd like water. A small gang of boys ran past, and a herd of pigeons rose from the square. It was hard to meet anyone in that throng, so we agreed to wait by the clock. In the end the train came, and the crowd went quiet.","zh":"车站外面聚了一大群人，好像还有更多人从各条街涌过来。我只好挤到前面去找我妹妹。一大批旅客都在等着那趟晚点列车的消息。后来有人带头欢呼，那声音像水一样在人群里漫开。一小帮男孩从旁边跑过，广场上一群鸽子飞了起来。在那么密的人堆里想找个人太难了，我们就说好在钟底下等。最后车来了，人群也静了下来。"},"cure":{"tw":[["heal",0],["dun",3],["change",2],["treat",6],["remedy",0],["recuperate",3],["help",2],["heart",6],["therapeutic",0]],"en":"The doctor said there was no quick cure for my grandfather's legs, only time. He could treat the pain, but he could not heal the bone. What would help most, he said, was rest and a slow walk each day. My grandmother had her own remedy: soup, quiet, and the sound of the radio. She believed the smell of the kitchen was therapeutic in itself. For weeks he would recuperate in the back room under a dun blanket. His heart was stronger than any of us had feared, and in spring he could change his own bandage. I sat with him every evening and read the paper aloud.","zh":"医生说爷爷的腿没有速效的治法，只能靠时间。他能止疼，却没法把骨头治好。他说最管用的是休息，外加每天慢慢走一走。我奶奶有她自己的方子：汤、安静，还有收音机的声音。她相信厨房里的味儿本身就有疗效。好几个星期，他都盖着一条灰褐色的毯子在里屋养着。他的心脏比我们任何人担心的都要强，到了春天已经能自己换绷带了。每天晚上我陪着他，把报纸读出声来。"},"calculate":{"tw":[["calculus",4],["bet",0],["balance",3],["intend",2],["predict",6],["count",0],["figure",0]],"en":"My brother had to calculate the cost of the trip before we left. He would count every meal and figure the price of the rooms, then try to predict how much we would spend on fuel. He is good with numbers and once studied calculus at school. I made a bet with him that he would get it wrong, but his balance was close to the truth. I did not intend to argue. In the end we had enough money, with a little left over. He was right, and I paid for the tea.","zh":"出发前，哥哥得把这趟的花费算清楚。他把每顿饭都算上，估出住店的钱，再猜油费要多少。他对数字很在行，在学校还学过微积分。我跟他打赌说他一定算错，结果他那个收支算得很接近实情。我本来也没想争。最后钱够用，还剩了一点。他赢了，我请了茶。"},"character":{"tw":[["characteristic",4],["quality",0],["heavy",3],["property",2],["personality",6],["letter",3],["scratch",2]],"en":"The main character in the story is a quiet man with a heavy past. The writer gives him one clear characteristic: he never speaks first. His personality comes out slowly, in small acts, and the quality of the writing makes you trust him. Every letter he writes is short. He lives alone on an old property at the edge of town, and a long scratch marks the door. He never says what he feels, and that is why the ending hurts. I liked him better than any other figure in the book.","zh":"故事里的主角是个背着沉重过往的沉默男人。作者只给他一个鲜明的特点：他从不先开口。他的性情一点点显出来，都在些小事里，文字的分量让你愿意信他。他写的每封信都很短。他独自住在城边一处旧宅里，门上一道长长的划痕。他从不说自己的感受，正因如此，那个结局才叫人难受。这本书里，我最喜欢他，胜过任何别的角色。"},"competition":{"tw":[["compete",4],["contest",0],["game",3],["event",6],["rival",0],["race",3],["tournament",6]],"en":"Every summer our town holds a small competition on the river. People come from other villages to compete in a boat race, and the winner takes a cup. The main event is the long swim, but the boat contest draws the biggest crowd. My sister had a rival she had beaten twice before, and this time the girl trained all spring. The whole tournament lasted three days, and the last game was decided by a single second. Nobody talked about anything else for weeks. She came second, and she smiled anyway.","zh":"每年夏天，我们镇上都在河边办一场小比赛。别村的人来做赛船，赢的人拿一只杯子。主要项目是长距离游泳，可围观人最多的是划船赛。姐姐有个对手，她赢过人家两回，这回那姑娘练了一整个春天。整个赛程持续三天，最后一场就靠一秒定输赢。后来好几个星期，大家嘴里只有这件事。姐姐拿了第二，可她还是笑了。"},"criminal":{"tw":[["crime",4],["crook",0],["principal",2],["murder",6],["outlaw",0],["trial",6],["felon",0]],"en":"The man they caught was a criminal with a long record. He had once been part of a gang of crooks, and people said he was the principal figure in a murder near the old bridge. The police called him an outlaw and a felon, and the trial lasted a month. In the end, the judge said the crime was serious, but that no one should be treated as less than human. He went to prison and wrote letters home. Years later, he came back to the town and worked in a garden.","zh":"他们抓到的那个人是个前科累累的罪犯。他以前是一伙骗子的成员，人们说老桥附近那桩命案他是主犯。警方称他是逃犯、重罪犯，审判拖了一个月。最后法官说罪行是严重的，但谁都不该被当成不是人来对待。他进了监狱，一直往家里写信。多年以后，他回到镇上，在园子里干活。"},"adapt":{"tw":[["accommodate",0],["fit",3],["change",2],["evolve",6],["adjust",3],["square",3],["integrate",6],["conform",0]],"en":"When the old tailor took on a young apprentice, the shop had to adapt. The bench did not fit him, so we had to adjust its height and accommodate his long arms. People change slowly, the tailor said, but they do evolve if you give them time. At first the boy tried to square everything with the ruler, and nothing would square. He wanted to conform to the old rules too quickly, and every coat came out wrong. Then he stopped copying and began to integrate what he knew. By winter his jackets fitted better than mine. He had learned to fit the cloth, not the rule.","zh":"老裁缝收了个年轻学徒，铺子就得跟着适应。工作台不合他的身量，我们只好调高度，将就他那两条长胳膊。裁缝说，人变得慢，可是给够时间，总会慢慢变。起初那孩子什么都想用尺子量得方方正正，结果怎么都不成方圆。他太急着去迎合老规矩，做出来的大衣件件不对。后来他不再照抄，开始把学到的东西融进去。到冬天，他做的外套比我做的还合身。他学会的是顺着布料去做，不是顺着规矩。"},"advise":{"tw":[["suggest",0],["move",3],["inform",2],["instruct",6],["propose",0],["advance",3],["ask",6],["counsel",0]],"en":"The manager called me in to advise me about the new shift. She wanted to inform me that the night work would start in June, and to suggest that I take two weeks off first. I had to ask whether the pay would change. She could not promise an advance, but she did propose a small rise after the first month. Then she began to instruct me in the details, slowly, as good counsel should. It was a bold move for her, and I respected it. I agreed to start on the first Monday. The work turned out to be harder than she said.","zh":"经理把我叫进去，就新的班次给我出主意。她想通知我夜班六月开始，也建议我先休两周。我得问清楚工资会不会变。她不敢保证给我预支，但确实提了第一个月之后小幅涨薪。接着她开始一点一点教我具体的活，好建议本来就该是这样。对她来说这是个大胆的举动，我领这个情。我答应第一个周一就去上工。这活儿后来比她说的要难。"},"amaze":{"tw":[["pose",0],["dazzle",3],["surprise",2],["fascinate",6],["puzzle",0],["confuse",2],["rejoice",6],["astonish",0]],"en":"The magician did not amaze us with tricks alone. He would pose a simple question, then dazzle the whole room with the answer, and the surprise on our faces was what he enjoyed most. The old clock on the table seemed to fascinate him more than the crowd did. One part of the act still puzzles me, and the way he hid the coin only confused my sister. When he finally made the bird appear, the children began to rejoice and shout. I have never seen a hall so astonished. He bowed, picked up his hat, and left without a word.","zh":"那位魔术师让我们惊叹的，不只是手法。他先抛出一个小问题，再用答案把满屋子人都晃花眼，我们脸上的那点意外，是他最受用的。桌上那座老钟好像比台下的人还让他着迷。有一个环节我到今天还琢磨不透，他藏硬币那一下只是把我妹妹绕晕了。等他最后变出那只鸟时，孩子们又叫又乐。我从没见过一屋子人那样吃惊。他鞠了个躬，拿起帽子，一句话没说就走了。"},"appreciate":{"tw":[["value",0],["see",2],["depreciate",1],["admire",6],["prize",0],["understand",2],["wish",6],["treasure",0]],"en":"My father knew how to appreciate a good tool. He would stand in the shop and admire a worn plane for ten minutes before he said anything. He prized the old ones most, though he did understand that a new car depreciates the moment you drive it off the lot. I did not always see what he saw in them, and I wish now that I had asked more. He told me to value the hands that made a thing, not the price on the tag. I still treasure the small hammer he left me. It is not worth much, but I would not sell it.","zh":"我爸懂得怎么欣赏一件好工具。他会在店里站上十分钟，端详一把用旧的刨子，才开口说话。他最看重那些老的，虽然他也明白新车一开出停车场就开始贬值。他眼里的好东西，我不总看得见，现在真希望当年多问几句。他跟我说，要看重做成一件东西的那双手，不是标签上的价钱。他留给我的那把小锤子，我一直珍藏着。它值不了多少钱，可我舍不得卖。"},"argue":{"tw":[["reason",0],["oppose",3],["present",2],["insist",6],["fence",0],["defend",3],["disagree",6],["contend",0]],"en":"The two neighbours had argued about the fence for years. One would present his reasons calmly, and the other would simply insist that he was right. They could disagree about a single post for a whole afternoon. My father took the first man's side and tried to defend him, but he would never oppose the second to his face. I watched from the kitchen window as they went on and on. In the end the older man said he would contend with the matter in the spring. Nobody moved a board. That evening they shared the same bench by the gate.","zh":"那两家邻居为这道篱笆争了好些年。一个总是平静地摆出自己的理由，另一个就一口咬定自己没错。为一根柱子他们能吵一整个下午。我爸站第一个人那边，想替他说话，可他当面从不去反驳第二个人。我趴在厨房窗子上看他们没完没了地说。最后那位年长的说他春天再来理论。谁也没有挪动一块木板。那天傍晚，两个人又坐在了门口同一条长凳上。"},"assure":{"tw":[["insure",0],["cover",3],["calm",2],["ensure",6],["secure",0],["contract",3],["affirm",2],["intend",6]],"en":"The agent had to assure the young couple that the house was safe to buy. He could insure it against fire and flood, and the contract would cover the roof as well. To ensure there were no surprises, he walked them through every line twice. The wife asked him to affirm one point in writing, and he did. He wanted them to feel secure about the money they were about to spend. She grew calm and signed. He wrote his name at the bottom and handed her a pen. I intend to call him myself when I buy next year.","zh":"那位业务员得让这对年轻夫妇放心，这房子可以买。他能给它保火险和水险，合同也会把屋顶包含进去。为了确保没有意外，他领着他们把每一条都过了两遍。妻子请他把其中一点写下来确认，他照做了。他想让他们对即将花出去的这笔钱有安全感。她放下心来，签了字。他写下自己的名字，递给她一支笔。明年我买房的时候，也打算找他。"},"atmosphere":{"tw":[["air",0],["spirit",3],["gas",2],["intense",6],["aura",0],["weather",2],["cool",6],["ambience",0]],"en":"The air inside the old greenhouse was warm and heavy, and the whole atmosphere changed the moment you stepped in from the cold. They had to pump a gas through the pipes to keep the plants alive, and the work was intense in July. The weather outside hardly mattered. What struck me most was the calm aura of the place, and the strange cool ambience of the far corner, where the ferns grew. Even the workers moved slowly there. It was, my aunt said, a good spirit to work in. I stayed until the last light went and the glass turned grey.","zh":"旧温室里的空气又暖又重，你一从冷风里跨进去，整间屋子给人的感觉就变了。他们得通过管子打进一种气体，让那些植物活下来，七月里这活儿很吃重。外面的天气几乎不相干。最打动我的是这个地方安静的气场，还有角落里长着蕨草的那一小片清凉气氛。连工人在那儿走路都放轻。我姑姑说，这是个干活的好心境。我一直待到天光散尽，玻璃变成灰色。"},"attract":{"tw":[["draw",0],["bring",3],["repel",1],["lure",6],["pull",2],["catch",3],["benefit",6],["appeal",0]],"en":"The little stall did not attract many people at first. My uncle tried to draw a crowd with a wooden sign, but the bright colours seemed to repel the older shoppers. He learned to lure them in with the smell of fresh bread, and that worked better than any shouting. Two women stopped to pull the door open and catch the warmth inside. There was a real benefit to the quiet hours, he said; the slow ones bought more. By noon the shop had an appeal I had not expected. I brought my friends there the next day, and they came back with me all winter.","zh":"那个小摊子起初招不来多少人。我叔叔想用一块木招牌把人引来，可那些鲜亮的颜色反倒把上了年纪的顾客挡在外头。他后来学会用新出炉面包的香味把人勾进门，比喊什么都管用。两位女士停下来，推开门去接屋里的暖气。他说，清闲的时段其实有好处，慢悠悠的客人买得更多。到中午，这家店有了一种我没想到的吸引力。第二天我领了几个朋友来，整个冬天他们都跟我来。"},"average":{"tw":[["fair",0],["median",3],["total",2],["turnover",6],["mean",3],["achieve",2],["rate",6],["ordinary",5]],"en":"My aunt kept the books for the small shop, and she liked to look at the average of every week. On an ordinary day the turnover was low, but the total for the month still pleased her. She would work out the mean of the costs and then the median, to see which figure was fair to use. The shop had to achieve a certain rate of profit to stay open. It was not a rich business, and the numbers never changed much. That steadiness, she said, was worth more than one good year. I helped her add the columns twice and made the same small mistake twice.","zh":"我姑姑给小店记账，喜欢看每一周的平均数。平常日子进账不多，可一个月加起来的总额还是让她满意。她会先算出成本的平均值，再算中位数，看用哪个数才公道。店要维持下去，得达到一定的利润率。这不是什么赚钱的生意，数字一直没多大变化。她说，这种平稳比某一个好年景值钱。我帮她核了两遍那几栏数，同一个错还犯了两回。"},"balance":{"tw":[["rest",0],["cancel",3],["hold",2],["strength",6],["proportion",0],["complement",3],["rather",6],["equilibrium",0]],"en":"My father could hold his balance on a ladder better than any man I knew. He would keep his strength in his legs and let his arms do the rest. If one board was too short he would cancel the order and start again, rather than force it. He said the proportion of a frame was like a person's complement, and that a good house needed both weight and equilibrium. I would rather have watched from the ground. He never once fell. Twice a day he stopped to stand still and steady himself. That habit kept him on his feet for forty years.","zh":"我爸在梯子上保持平衡的本事，我认识的人里没有比得上的。他把力气都放在两条腿上，让胳膊去管剩下的。木板短了，他会把订单退掉重来，而不是硬凑。他说一个架子的比例就像一个人的补足，一间好房子既要有分量，也要有平衡。我倒宁愿站在地上看。他一次也没摔过。每天两回，他会停下来站定，把自己稳住。这个习惯让他在工地上站了四十年。"},"bat":{"tw":[["lick",0],["paddle",3],["beat",2],["catch",6],["flutter",0],["club",2],["ball",6],["thrash",0]],"en":"At dusk we played with a wooden bat in the yard until the light went. My cousin would beat the ground with it twice before he swung, and then he would thrash at anything I threw. I was there to catch the ball, and I was not very good. Once, a bat came out from under the roof and began to flutter over our heads, and we all stopped to watch it. The dog tried to lick my hand and knocked the club over. Then we used a paddle as a second bat and played on in the dark. Nobody wanted to go in and do the dishes.","zh":"黄昏时我们在院里拿一根木棒打球，一直打到天光没了。我表哥挥棒前总要在地上磕两下，然后不管我扔什么他都狠狠抽。我负责接球，接得不太好。有一回，一只蝙蝠从屋檐下飞出来，在我们头顶上扑腾，我们都停下来看它。狗想舔我的手，一下把球棒碰倒了。后来我们把一把桨当第二根棒，摸黑接着打。谁也不想进屋去洗碗。"},"blame":{"tw":[["charge",2],["knock",2],["absolve",1],["fear",6],["pick",0],["reproach",2],["worry",6],["fault",0]],"en":"Nobody would say who had broken the window, and for two days the whole class was under a cloud of blame. The teacher had to pick one story and charge the boy she believed, though she had no proof. He sat with his head down, sick with fear, and I could see the worry on his face. She told him she did not want to reproach him, only to hear the truth. When the caretaker found the real mark on the frame, she had to absolve him at once. The knock had come from a falling branch. It was nobody's fault.","zh":"没人肯说是谁打碎了那扇窗，整整两天，全班都笼在一层说不清的责备里。老师只好挑一种说法，指控她信的那个男孩，其实她也没有证据。他低着头坐着，怕得厉害，我看得出他脸上的焦虑。她说她不想责骂他，只想听真话。看门人在窗框上找到了真正的痕迹，她只好立刻还他清白。那一下响，是断落的树枝砸的。谁都没有错。"},"bomb":{"tw":[["fail",0],["blast",3],["attack",2],["suicide",6],["bombard",0],["bombshell",3],["flop",2],["raid",6]],"en":"My grandfather told me once about the night a bomb fell near his street. There had been a raid, and the blast broke every window in the row. He said the attack failed to hit the bridge, and the planes went on to bombard the fields outside the town. A reporter later wrote that the whole thing was a flop, and for his family it was a bombshell that came without warning. He would not speak of the suicide of the young man next door. Some parts of that story, he said, had to be left out. I did not ask him again.","zh":"爷爷跟我讲过一次，那天夜里一颗炸弹落在他家那条街附近。先是空袭，爆炸震碎了整排房子的窗子。他说那次袭击没能炸中大桥，飞机转去轰炸城外的田地。后来有个记者写，这整件事是场失败；而对他家来说，那是个毫无预兆落下来的晴天霹雳。他不肯提隔壁那个年轻人自杀的事。他说，这个故事有些部分只能不讲。我没有再问。"},"boom":{"tw":[["nail",0],["revive",3],["grow",2],["bubble",6],["roar",0],["hit",2],["slide",6],["smash",0]],"en":"There was a boom in the little town that year, and every carpenter had more work than he could take. My uncle would nail boards all morning and still be behind. He watched the market grow, then slide, then grow again, and he said the whole thing felt like a bubble. The old mill came back to life when a young family bought it and began to revive the trade. At noon we heard a roar from the street as a lorry hit the low bridge and seemed about to smash it. Nobody was hurt. The noise died, and by evening the town was quiet again.","zh":"那一年小镇上生意兴隆，每个木匠手里的活都忙不完。我叔叔一上午钉板子，还是赶不上进度。他看着市面涨上去，又滑下来，再涨回去，他说这一整套就像个气泡。那家老磨坊也活了过来，一户年轻人把它买下，慢慢把那门手艺重新做起来。中午街上轰的一声，一辆卡车撞上矮桥，看样子差点把它撞碎。没人受伤。响声过去了，到傍晚，镇上又安静下来。"},"boot":{"tw":[["charge",0],["punt",3],["blow",2],["heel",6],["kick",2],["shell",2],["hat",6],["bang",0]],"en":"The rain had turned the field into deep mud, and my boots were heavy with it before half time. The captain would punt the ball high and then run, and once he took a bad kick and fell on his heel. The referee gave a short blow on his whistle, a bang that made us all jump. There was no charge for the game, and the crowd stood under the old shell of the stand, holding their hats against the wind. Afterwards we sat in the shed and laughed at each other. Every one of us had one boot full of water.","zh":"雨把球场泡成了烂泥，半场还没到，我的靴子里就灌满了沉甸甸的泥。队长总把球高高踢出去再追，有一次他踢歪了，一下子摔到了脚跟上。裁判短促地吹了一声哨，那一声脆响把我们都吓了一跳。这场球不收钱，看台上的人挤在旧看台的骨架底下，按着帽子挡风。散场后我们坐在棚子里互相取笑。我们每个人，都有一只靴子里全是水。"},"branch":{"tw":[["arm",0],["twig",3],["grow",2],["section",6],["leg",0],["stream",2],["main",6],["separate",0]],"en":"The old tree by the stream had one main branch that reached right over the water, and from it grew a hundred smaller twigs. My grandfather would sit in his chair under it, one arm on the wooden arm of the seat, his bad leg stretched out on a stone. He liked to watch the separate streams join below the section of bank he had planted himself. If a branch broke, he never threw it away; he kept every piece. A shop in the next town was the other branch of his family, he said, and both had grown from the same root.","zh":"溪边那棵老树有一根主枝一直伸到水面上，上面又长出上百根细枝。爷爷总坐在树下的椅子上，一只手搭在木头扶手上，那条坏腿搁在一块石头上。他喜欢看几股分开的水在他亲手栽的那段河岸下面汇到一起。有树枝断了，他从不扔掉，每一段都留着。他说，邻镇那家铺子是家里另一根枝，两边都是从同一个根上长起来的。"},"brave":{"tw":[["weather",0],["defy",2],["timid",1],["courage",6],["endure",0],["warrior",2],["cry",6],["hardy",0]],"en":"My grandmother was a brave woman, though she never called herself one. She had the courage to weather three hard winters on that hill, and she would defy anyone who told her to move down to the town. She was not timid, and she was certainly hardy; she could endure cold that made the rest of us cry. Her husband had been a soldier, a quiet warrior who never spoke of it. When the storm took the roof, she stood in the yard and laughed. We rebuilt it together in the spring. She said the wind had only done half the work.","zh":"我奶奶是个勇敢的人，虽然她从不这么称呼自己。她有勇气在那座山冈上熬过三个严冬，谁劝她搬到镇上去，她都敢顶回去。她一点都不怯，身子也硬朗，那种冷我们冻得直哭，她受得住。她丈夫当过兵，是个从不提这些事的沉默战士。风暴掀了屋顶，她站在院子里笑。春天我们一起把房子重新盖了起来。她说，风只替我们干了一半的活。"},"breathe":{"tw":[["rest",0],["radiate",3],["express",2],["suck",6],["emit",0],["sigh",3],["exhaust",2],["breath",6]],"en":"The nurse asked him to sit and breathe slowly, and to take one deep breath after another. The old stove seemed to radiate a heavy heat that made the small room hard to bear. He had to suck the air through a tube, and it made him sigh with every turn. His illness would express itself as a dry cough, she said, and it could exhaust a man of his age. We were told to let him rest for an hour. The machine by the bed began to emit a soft, steady sound, and he closed his eyes. I counted the sound until it slowed me down too.","zh":"护士让他坐下来慢慢呼吸，一次接一次地深吸气。那座旧炉子好像往外散着一股闷热，让小屋子格外难受。他得用一根管子把气吸进来，每转一次就叹一声。她说，他这病会表现成干咳，而且能把一个他这岁数的人耗空。他们让我们让他歇上一个钟头。床边的仪器开始发出一种轻而平稳的声音，他闭上了眼睛。我数着那声音，慢慢地自己也被它哄慢了。"},"champion":{"tw":[["friend",0],["confederate",3],["back",2],["compatriot",6],["star",0],["advocate",2],["title",6],["ace",0]],"en":"When our team won the local cup, the whole street came out for the champion. He was not a great star, and he never behaved like one, but he had an old friend and confederate on the wing who fed him every ball. The crowd began to back him from the first minute, and a compatriot from his own village held up a flag. His coach, an advocate of plain, hard play, would not let him take all the credit. The title was theirs for one year. It was an ace of a season, and nobody has forgotten it. The cup still stands in the shop window.","zh":"我们队赢下本地杯赛那天，整条街都出来看这位冠军。他算不上什么大明星，也从不摆明星的架子，但边路上有个老朋友兼伙伴，每个球都传给他。看台上的人从第一分钟就替他使劲，一个同村的老乡还举起了一面旗。他的教练主张踏踏实实硬打，不肯让他一个人把功劳都揽了。这个头衔归他们一年。那是个了不起的赛季，谁也没忘。奖杯至今还摆在那家店的橱窗里。"},"chew":{"tw":[["jaw",0],["crunch",3],["grate",2],["gum",6],["plug",0],["mumble",3],["pant",6],["quid",0]],"en":"The old man at the stall would chew slowly, with his jaw moving like a machine, and never stop talking. He liked to crunch a piece of hard bread and then mumble about the price. His small stove would grate against the pan as he turned it, and he kept a plug of cheese on the board for his customers. He sold sweets and gum to the children and charged a few quid for the rest. Once, out of breath, he had to pant for a moment before he could go on. Nobody minded the wait. His bread was worth the noise and the wait.","zh":"摊子上的老人嚼东西很慢，下巴像台机器一样动着，嘴却从没停下。他爱咬一块硬面包，然后嘟囔着价钱。他翻动小炉子的时候，锅底和炉子磨得直响；案板上总放着一块奶酪给客人尝。他卖糖和口香糖给孩子，剩下的收几个英镑。有一回他喘不上气，得先大喘一会儿才能接着说话。谁也没嫌等得久。他的面包，值得听那些动静，也值得等。"},"comment":{"tw":[["notice",0],["knock",3],["report",2],["interview",6],["remark",0],["talk",3],["anonymity",6],["input",0]],"en":"The paper asked me to write a short comment on the new road, so I read every report and notice I could find. I gave one interview to a young writer who promised me anonymity, and I let him use part of our talk. His first remark was gentle, but the editor wanted some input from the people on the street. There came a knock at my door that evening, and it was a neighbour with a different view. I added his words the next morning. In the end the piece ran to two lines. Nobody wrote in, and I took the paper home.","zh":"报社让我就那条新路写一段短评，我把能找到的报道和告示都读了一遍。有个年轻记者给我做了一次采访，答应隐去我的名字，我让他用了我们谈话的一部分。他开头那句评论很温和，可编辑想要街上普通人说点什么。那天晚上有人敲我的门，是位看法不同的邻居。第二天早上我把他的话加了进去。最后这篇文章只有两行。没有人来信，我把报纸带回了家。"},"complicate":{"tw":[["elaborate",0],["involve",3],["change",2],["hamper",6],["refine",0],["develop",3],["derail",6],["perplex",0]],"en":"The simple job began to complicate the moment we changed the design. To involve the whole team in the fitting only seemed to slow us down, and a late delivery of steel would hamper every step. My uncle wanted to elaborate on his first plan, adding one small part after another, until even he looked perplexed. We had to refine the frame twice before it was truly square. Then a broken tool derailed the whole week, and we lost three days. In the end we built the plain version, and it worked better than the clever one. The idea itself did develop, but not in the way he wanted.","zh":"我们一改设计，这件简单的活儿就开始变复杂。让整队人都掺合进装配，反倒拖慢了进度；钢材晚到，每一步都受阻。我叔叔想把他最初那套方案再铺开讲，一个零件接一个零件地加，最后连他自己都看糊涂了。我们把架子返了两遍才算真正方正。接着一把工具坏了，把整整一周都带偏，白丢了三天。最后我们做了最朴素的那一版，它比那个花哨的版本好用。那个想法其实确实往前长了，只是没按他想的那个方向。"},"confuse":{"tw":[["fox",0],["bother",3],["embarrass",2],["intimidate",6],["blur",0],["distract",3],["annoy",6],["obscure",0]],"en":"The last question on the paper was meant to confuse us, and it did. The wording seemed to fox even the best student in the room. A loud clock on the wall began to bother me, and my tired eyes would blur the small print. I tried not to let the quiet of the hall intimidate me, but it did not help. One boy's pen kept tapping, which was enough to distract anyone and annoy the teacher. The instructions only served to obscure the real task. When I finally understood, I was too embarrassed to ask for more time. I handed in the sheet with half of it empty.","zh":"卷子最后一道题就是用来把人绕晕的，它确实做到了。那措辞连班里最好的学生都给难住了。墙上那口钟声音很响，开始扰得我心烦，眼睛也累得把那行小字看花。我努力不让考场那点安静吓住自己，可一点用都没有。一个男生的笔一直敲，足以让任何人分神，也把老师惹恼了。那些说明只是把真正的题目遮得更糊。等我终于看明白，我已经羞得不好意思再要时间。交卷的时候，那张纸空了一半。"},"contribute":{"tw":[["add",0],["combine",3],["change",2],["benefit",6],["bring",0],["pay",2],["achieve",6],["lead",0]],"en":"Everyone on our street was asked to contribute something to the little garden at the corner. My mother would add a few plants each spring, and my father would bring old tools from the shed. We had to combine our effort with the neighbours' if we wanted to change that bare patch of ground, and the benefit showed in a single summer. Nobody was paid, and nobody expected to be. By August we had begun to achieve something the council had not managed in years. One good row of beans can lead a whole street to talk to each other.","zh":"街上每户人家都被请着给街角那块小园子出点力。妈妈每年春天添几株花草，爸爸从棚子里搬来旧工具。想把那片光秃秃的地改个样，我们就得把手上的活跟邻居合到一处，一个夏天就看出了好处。没有人拿钱，也没人指望拿钱。到八月，我们做成了一件村委会好些年都没做成的事。一排长好的豆子，就能让整条街的人互相说上话。"},"crisp":{"tw":[["chip",0],["pucker",3],["heat",2],["sauce",6],["sharp",0],["fold",2],["glaze",6],["toast",0]],"en":"The bread came out of the oven with a crisp, dark crust that broke with a sharp sound under the knife. My aunt would fold the dough twice and let the heat do the rest. She brushed a thin glaze over the top and watched it shine. One batch went too far and turned into a hard chip, but the next was ready to toast by the window. The smell made my mouth pucker before I had even tasted it. We ate it with the last of the tomato sauce. I have never had better. The whole kitchen smelled of it for two days.","zh":"面包出炉，外壳又脆又深，刀子切下去，咔嚓一声就裂了。姑姑会把面团折上两回，剩下的交给炉温。她在顶上刷一层薄薄的亮浆，看着它发亮。有一炉烤过了头，硬得像块瓦片；下一炉摆到窗边，已经烤得正好。我还没尝，鼻子就先让口水冒了出来。我们就着最后一点番茄酱把它吃了。我从来没吃过更好的。那股香味在厨房里留了两天。"},"crown":{"tw":[["top",2],["pinnacle",3],["cover",2],["throne",6],["queen",6],["cap",0],["head",2],["jacket",0]],"en":"In the old museum, a golden crown sat on a small stand, and above it a glass cover kept the dust off. It had once belonged to a queen whose throne stood in the same hall. The guide said the crown was the top of their whole collection, the pinnacle of the goldsmith's art, and the head of every visitor turned to it at once. A small cap, bright blue, had been found beside it in the grave. I read the jacket of the museum book that evening. It was the one thing I wrote about that week.","zh":"旧博物馆里，一顶金冠搁在小台座上，上面罩着玻璃盖挡灰。它原先属于一位女王，她的王座就摆在同一个厅里。讲解员说，这顶王冠是他们整个收藏的最高处，是金匠手艺的顶点，每位参观者的头都会立刻转向它。它旁边还出土了一顶亮蓝色的小帽子。那天晚上我读了那本博物馆书的护封。那一周我写下的，就只有它。"},"accuse":{"tw":[["charge",0],["blame",3],["punish",6],["impeach",0],["indict",3],["allege",6],["incriminate",0]],"en":"A local paper began to accuse the head of the school board of taking money for repairs that were never done. The reporter would allege that some papers had been changed, though he could not prove it. Some neighbours wanted to charge the man at once, while others said he should not be blamed before a court looked at the facts. A lawyer said the board could not impeach him without a proper hearing, and that any move to indict him would need stronger proof. In the end nobody came forward to incriminate anyone, and the whole matter was quietly dropped. The board chose to punish no one.","zh":"本地一家报纸开始指责校董会主席拿钱做了根本没修的活。记者扬言有几份文件被人改过，可他又证明不了。有邻居想马上控告那个人，也有人说法院还没看事实之前不该怪他。一位律师说，不开正式的听证会，董事会没法罢免他；要起诉他也得有更硬的证据。最后没人站出来指控谁，整件事悄悄就搁下了。董事会决定不罚任何人。"},"ace":{"tw":[["single",0],["pass",2],["triple",6],["star",0],["play",2],["double",6],["champion",0]],"en":"On the last night of the holiday we would play cards at a small table on the porch. My uncle was the champion of the family at every game, a real star with a pack in his hand. He could pass a single card across the board so slowly that you never saw it move. Once he held an ace, then a double, then a triple, and still lost to my sister. Nobody could tell how she did it. Years later, when he was sick, he asked for one more game, and nobody had the heart to say no. We played until the lamp went out.","zh":"假期的最后一晚，我们都在门廊的小桌边打牌。我叔是全家每样牌戏的冠军，手里一叠牌就跟明星一样。他能把一张牌慢慢推过桌面，慢得你根本看不出它在动。有一次他手里拿了张幺点，接着又凑双、凑三，最后还是输给了我姐。谁也说不上她是怎么赢的。多年后他病了，还说要再来一局，谁都不忍心说不。我们一直打到灯灭。"},"annoy":{"tw":[["irritate",0],["harass",3],["displease",2],["terrify",6],["vex",0],["fret",3],["confuse",6]],"en":"The new neighbours played loud music every night, and it began to annoy the whole street. The sound would irritate my mother most, because she had to get up early. My father tried to be calm, but the endless knocking on the wall would vex him in the end. He did not want to harass them with complaints, and he hated to displease anyone. My little brother, who was afraid of the dark, found the noise enough to terrify him. So my mother would fret all evening, and the mixed messages from each of us only seemed to confuse the matter. In the end we simply asked them, politely, once.","zh":"新搬来的邻居每晚都放很响的音乐，整条街都开始受不了。我妈最被吵得烦，因为她得早起。我爸想稳住，可墙上没完没了的敲击到底还是把他惹恼了。他不想拿抱怨去缠人家，也最不愿惹人不高兴。我小弟怕黑，那声音足以把他吓坏。于是我妈一晚上发愁，我们各说各的话，反倒把这事搅得更乱。最后我们只是客客气气地去说了一次。"},"appoint":{"tw":[["charge",0],["create",3],["decide",6],["constitute",0],["empower",3],["designate",6],["nominate",0]],"en":"When the old foreman retired, the company had to appoint someone to take charge of the night team. The manager had already decided to create a new post rather than fill the empty one. Two names were put forward: one man nominated by the workers, and one woman the office wished to designate as head. The rules did not empower the manager to choose alone; a small group had to vote. In the end they chose the woman, and together they began to constitute a fresh way of running the floor. It was a small change, but everyone felt it by the first week.","zh":"老工头退休后，公司得指派一个人来管夜班这一摊。经理已经决定，与其补上空缺，不如新设一个岗位。有两个名字被提了出来：一个是工人们推举的男人，一个是办公室想指定当头的女人。规矩并没有授权经理一个人说了算，得由一个小班子投票。最后他们选了那个女人，然后一起着手搭起了一套新的车间管理办法。变化不大，可头一个星期大家都感觉得到。"},"arrest":{"tw":[["catch",0],["attract",2],["trial",6],["check",0],["capture",2],["custody",6],["hold",0]],"en":"The police had to arrest a young man after a shop was broken into at night. They would check every camera on the street before they could catch him. The case seemed to attract a lot of talk in the small town, and a few people wanted him kept in custody until the trial. In the end it took the officers three days to capture him at a bus stop. He was held for a week, then let go when no proof could be found. The shopkeeper said the whole affair had only taught him to lock his back door.","zh":"夜里一家店被闯了之后，警方不得不逮捕一个年轻人。他们得把街上每个摄像头都查一遍，才能抓住他。这案子在这座小镇上引来了不少议论，有几个人希望审判之前先把他关着。最后几名警员花了三天，在一个公交站把他逮住。他被关了一个星期，因为查不到证据又放了。店主说，这整件事只教会他一件事：把后门锁好。"},"article":{"tw":[["clause",0],["paper",3],["piece",2],["page",6],["feature",3],["section",2],["book",6]],"en":"My mother wrote a short article for the local paper every month. It was always one piece of about six hundred words, and it usually ran on the same page. That winter she was asked to write a feature about the old market, which would take up a whole section of the Sunday book review. She would read the contract carefully, line by line, and once made them change a clause about who owned the words. She said a writer should never sign anything without reading it twice. I still keep a copy of that article in a drawer. The paper folded years ago, but the words are still there.","zh":"我妈每个月给本地报纸写一篇短文。总是一篇六百字左右的东西，通常登在同一版上。那年冬天，有人请她写一篇关于老集市的特稿，要占掉周日书评的整版。她会一行一行地细读合同，还有一次让他们改掉了一条关于文章归谁的条款。她说，写东西的人不该不读两遍就签字。那篇文章的剪报我至今收在抽屉里。报纸多年前就停了，可那些字还在。"},"attitude":{"tw":[["position",3],["guard",3],["cognition",2],["stance",6],["posture",0],["pose",3],["manner",6]],"en":"My sister went to a job interview with an attitude I had never seen in her before. She sat with a straight posture and a steady manner, and when they asked about her last job she did not go on the guard. The manager wanted to know her position on working late, and she kept the same calm stance throughout. Fear, she said later, had clouded her cognition for the first minute. Still, she had taken the time to pose one careful question of her own. She got the job. I think the firmness of her manner did as much as her answers.","zh":"我姐去面试时，带着一种我从没在她身上见过的态度。她坐姿端正，举止沉稳；人家问起她上一份工作时，她也没有防备起来。经理想知道她对加班的立场，她从头到尾都保持着那份平静的姿态。后来她说，是害怕让她头一分钟脑子有点转不动。可她到底还是抽空提了一个自己准备好的问题。她拿到了这份工作。我想她举止里的那份笃定，跟她的回答一样管用。"},"bang":{"tw":[["bed",0],["blow",2],["hum",6],["charge",0],["close",2],["peep",6],["hit",2]],"en":"At midnight the wind gave the old window a bang that woke the whole house. My father got up to close it, and the shutters took another blow before he could reach them. I could hear the kettle begin to hum in the kitchen, and someone gave a peep round the door, afraid to come in. He had to charge up the stairs to check on my grandmother in bed. The rain would hit the roof in long waves. Nobody slept much that night, and in the morning the garden was full of broken branches. The window never shut properly after that.","zh":"半夜里风把旧窗户撞得砰一下，把一家人都吵醒了。我爸起来关窗，还没够着，百叶又挨了一下。我听见厨房里的水壶开始嗡嗡响，有人扒着门边往里看一眼，不敢进来。他只好几步冲上楼，去看躺在床上的奶奶。雨一阵一阵地砸在屋顶上。那一夜谁也没睡踏实，早上院子里落满了断枝。从那以后那扇窗就再也没关严过。"},"bark":{"tw":[["skin",0],["bay",3],["cover",2],["pine",6],["cinnamon",3],["cry",2],["twig",6]],"en":"Our dog would bark at anything that moved near the fence, even a twig falling from the pine above the gate. Under its rough skin of bark the old tree was full of cracks, and a thin smell of cinnamon came from it in autumn. The dog liked to bay at the moon and would not stop until my mother called him in. He would then lie on a cover by the door and give a small cry in his sleep. Once he ran off to the bay at the end of the road and came back muddy and happy.","zh":"我们那条狗，只要篱笆边有什么动，它就汪汪叫，连门口松树上掉根小枝也照样。那棵老树粗糙的树皮下满是裂纹，秋天还透出一股淡淡的肉桂味。狗爱对着月亮长吠，我妈不叫它进来它就不停。进屋后它趴在门边的毯子上，睡梦里还小声呜咽。有一次它跑到路尽头那片海湾，回来时浑身是泥，却高兴得很。"},"basket":{"tw":[["hoop",0],["frail",3],["goal",2],["ball",6],["hamper",3],["score",2],["throw",6]],"en":"The old woman carried her shopping home in a frail basket with a broken hoop at the top. It was full of fruit that day, and one heavy hamper of potatoes sat beside it. On the way she stopped to watch a game of ball in the park. A boy would throw and the others would cheer, and every score was marked on a small board. She said the only goal that mattered to her was to reach home before the rain. She made it, just, though the basket bent a little more on each step. Her hands remembered every dent in the handle.","zh":"老太太用一个口沿上箍圈都断了的破篮子把买的东西拎回家。那天篮里装满了水果，旁边还搁着一大篮沉甸甸的土豆。路上她停下来看公园里的一场球赛。一个男孩投球，其他人就喊好，每次得分都记在一块小板上。她说对自己来说，唯一要紧的目标就是在下雨前到家。她刚好赶上，只是每走一步篮子就弯得更厉害一点。她的两只手记得把手上每一处凹痕。"},"beef":{"tw":[["kick",0],["complain",2],["pork",6],["crab",0],["meat",2],["veal",6],["gripe",0]],"en":"The butcher had no beef that morning, only pork, a little veal, and a few crabs in a box. A customer began to complain that the prices had gone up again, and would gripe about the meat for ten minutes. The butcher took it well and did not kick up a fuss. He said the whole trade had changed since the spring, and there was little he could do. In the end she bought some pork, and he threw in a small crab for free. She left happier than she came. He said a good word costs nothing and keeps the door open.","zh":"那天早上肉铺没有牛肉，只有猪肉、一点小牛肉，箱子里还有几只蟹。一位顾客开始抱怨价钱又涨了，对着那些肉念叨了十分钟。屠夫不在意，也没闹脾气。他说今年开春以来整个行当都变了，他也做不了什么。最后她买了些猪肉，他还白送了她一只小蟹。她走的时候比来时高兴。他说，一句好话不要钱，还能把门留着开。"},"blank":{"tw":[["clean",0],["character",2],["box",6],["space",0],["sheet",2],["copy",6],["white",0]],"en":"She sat at the table with a clean sheet of white paper and a pen, but the page stayed blank. There was a small box at the top where she had to write her name, one character at a time. Below it was a wide space for the story, and she had no idea what to put there. She made a copy of the old form first, to keep the first one safe. Then she began, slowly, to fill the lines. By evening the sheet was heavy with words, and the blank box at the top was the last thing she finished.","zh":"她坐在桌前，面前是一张干净的白纸和一支笔，可那页纸一直空着。顶上有个小方框，她得在那儿一个字一个字写下自己的名字。下面是一大片留给故事的地方，她却不知道该写什么。她先把旧表格复印了一份，把原件收好。然后她开始一行一行慢慢填。到傍晚，纸已经被字压得很沉，而顶上那个空格是她最后才填完的。"},"bless":{"tw":[["consecrate",0],["raise",2],["curse",1],["pray",6],["hallow",0],["god",6],["sanctify",0]],"en":"On the first Sunday of harvest, the old priest would bless the bread before anyone ate. He would raise both hands and pray over it, and the whole room went quiet. People said the building had been hallowed long ago, and that the same words were used to consecrate a new bell each time one was made. My grandmother would whisper that a blessing and a curse come from the same mouth, so one should be careful what one says. She believed a good life could sanctify the smallest thing. She never spoke of God as a distant figure, but as someone near the table.","zh":"收获后的第一个礼拜天，老神父会在大家吃东西前先为面包祝福。他会举起双手对着它祷告，整间屋子都安静下来。人们说这座房子很久以前就被祝圣过，每次铸新钟，用的还是那套话把它圣化。奶奶会小声说，祝福和诅咒出自同一张嘴，所以说话得留神。她相信，好好过日子能把最不起眼的东西也变得神圣。她从不说上帝离得很远，只说他就坐在桌边。"},"blind":{"tw":[["screen",2],["curtain",3],["change",2],["man",6],["dim",0],["dazzle",3],["woman",6]],"en":"The man pulled down the blind so the room would be shut off from the street, then drew the curtain across the far window. The lamp was dim, and a screen had been set up at the end where a small film would play. A woman in the back row said the light from the machine would dazzle her, so they moved it a little. When the film ended, the change from dark to bright was so sudden that everyone blinked. Nobody wanted to pull the blind open again. In the quiet that followed, the man said he could sit there all night. Outside, the street had turned very cold.","zh":"那个男人拉下百叶帘，把屋子跟街上隔开，又把窗帘拉过里面那扇窗。灯很暗，屋子那头支起了一块幕布，要放一部小片子。后排一个女人说机器上的光会晃得她眼花，于是他们把灯挪了挪。片子放完，由暗到亮变得太突然，人人都眨了眨眼。谁也不想再把帘子拉开。随后的一片安静里，那个男人说，他能在那里坐一整夜。外面，街上已经很冷了。"},"bloom":{"tw":[["flower",0],["bud",3],["develop",2],["hardy",6],["prime",0],["lily",6],["peak",0]],"en":"In late spring the garden behind our house was in full bloom. The first bud had opened in April, and by May the place was a sea of flower. My mother liked the white lily best; she said it was hardy and needed almost no care. She would watch a plant develop from a single leaf to its peak in a few short weeks. In its prime, the whole bed looked as if it had been painted. Then one cold night took it all, and we waited a year for the next one. She only said it would all come back in time.","zh":"春末时，屋后的花园开得正盛。第一朵花苞四月就开了，到了五月，那地方就是一片花海。我妈最爱那株白百合；她说它耐活，几乎不用管。她会看着一棵植物在短短几周里，从一片叶子长到最盛的样子。全盛时，整片花圃像被谁画过一样。后来一个寒夜把花都打掉了，我们又等了一年。她只说，到时候一切还会回来。"},"boss":{"tw":[["chief",0],["block",3],["impress",2],["former",6],["stamp",0],["real",6],["knob",0]],"en":"The former chief of our workshop came back for one day to see the old machines. He was the boss who had first taught me how to turn a knob and read a dial, and he could still impress the younger men with one quick look at a faulty part. A metal block on the bench had his name and a small stamp on it, worn smooth by years of hands. He said a real foreman never stands still; he walks the floor. When he left, nobody spoke for a while. We all stood a little straighter for the rest of that week.","zh":"我们车间以前的头儿回来待了一天，看看那些老机器。他就是当年教我拧旋钮、看表盘的老板，如今往坏零件上看一眼，还是能让年轻人服气。工作台上有一块铁块，上面刻着他的名字和一枚小小的印记，被多年的手摸得发亮。他说，真正的工头从不站着不动，他得在车间里走。他走后，好一会儿没人说话。那一个星期剩下的日子，我们站得都直了些。"},"brand":{"tw":[["mark",2],["label",3],["call",2],["apparel",6],["post",0],["sell",6],["steel",0]],"en":"Above the door of the small shop hung a sign with a simple brand on it, a mark my grandfather had drawn himself. The label on every coat said the same word, and people would call it the best apparel in the street. He would post a new price list each Monday and sell whatever the season asked for. He once showed me a steel ruler he had used for forty years. He said a brand is not a name you print; it is a promise you keep when nobody is watching. I still pass that door on my way home.","zh":"小店的门头上挂着一块牌子，上面是一个简单的牌子标志，是我爷爷亲手画的。每件外套上的标签都写着同一个词，人们都称它是这条街上最好的服装。他每周一贴出新的价目表，什么季节卖什么。有一回他给我看了一把用了四十年的钢尺。他说，牌子不是你印上去的名字，而是没人看着时你仍然守着的承诺。我如今回家路上还会经过那扇门。"},"breast":{"tw":[["front",0],["reach",2],["skin",6],["chest",0],["converge",2],["prostate",6],["summit",0]],"en":"The doctor sat on the front edge of the desk and asked my uncle to take a deep breath. He wanted to reach the chest area and check the skin below the left breast, where a small mark had appeared. Three roads of worry seemed to converge in that one room that morning. He said the summit of the whole visit was not the picture, but the words they would use to explain it. He also asked a few questions about the prostate, because my uncle was over sixty. Then he smiled and said, for now, all looked well. My uncle let out a long breath he had been holding.","zh":"医生坐在桌子最靠外的一角，让我叔深吸一口气。他要够到胸口那一带，查看左胸下面一小块皮肤，那里新出现了一个小印子。那天早上，几路担忧似乎都汇到了那一间屋里。他说，这趟看病最要紧的地方不在那张片子，而在他们最后用来说明的那些话。因为叔叔过了六十，他还问了几句关于前列腺的问题。然后他笑着说，就目前看，一切都还好。我叔把一直憋着的那口气长长吐了出来。"},"breed":{"tw":[["cover",0],["cause",2],["species",6],["stock",0],["produce",2],["herd",6],["strain",0]],"en":"On the hill farm they kept a whole herd of sheep of one old breed, and a second stock of cows beside them. My uncle could name every species in the valley and would cover a long page with notes on each one. He said the strain of a good animal was not luck but years of care. Bad weather could cause real harm, and a sick beast could produce nothing for a season. He would walk the field at dusk and count them twice, because a lost one was a loss for the whole herd. That farm taught me more than any book.","zh":"在山坡上的那个农场里，他们养着整整一群老品种的羊，旁边还有另一批牛。我叔能叫出山谷里每一种物种的名字，还会为每一种写满一整页笔记。他说，一头好牲畜的血统不是运气，而是多年的用心。坏天气会带来真正的伤害，一头病畜一整个季节什么也产不出来。傍晚他会走遍牧场，把牲口数上两遍，因为丢了一头，就是整群都亏了。那座农场教我的，比任何一本书都多。"},"bridge":{"tw":[["span",0],["contract",3],["join",2],["tunnel",6],["auction",3],["support",2],["along",6]],"en":"The old bridge over the river could no longer carry the weight it once did. Engineers came to measure its span and to decide how to support the two stone ends. They said a new contract would be needed before any work could begin. Some wanted to join the two banks with a wider deck, others preferred a tunnel under the water. A public auction of the old iron parts was even suggested, to help pay for the job. For now, people still walk along the wooden path on one side and look down at the water. Nobody is in a hurry to lose it.","zh":"河上那座老桥再也撑不住它以前能撑的重量了。工程师来量它的跨度，商量怎么托住两头的石墩。他们说，动工之前得先签一份新合同。有人想用更宽的桥面把两岸接起来，也有人宁愿在水下挖一条隧道。甚至有人提议把旧铁件公开拍卖，用来补贴这笔工程。眼下人们还是从一侧的木道走过去，低头看水。谁也不急着把它拆掉。"},"cable":{"tw":[["line",0],["television",2],["network",6],["wire",0],["rope",2],["channel",6],["telegraph",0]],"en":"The village got its first television signal the year the men ran a long cable up the mountain. Before that, the only line to the outside world was a thin telegraph wire that often failed in the snow. A young engineer had to climb the rock face and fix the rope himself, hand over hand. Once the network was up, every house could pick a different channel, and the children stayed in all evening. My grandmother said she preferred the old radio, but she watched with us anyway. That cable line changed more than any road ever had. She still keeps her small radio by the bed.","zh":"村里第一次收到电视信号，是那年男人们把一根长电缆拉上山的时候。在那之前，通往外面世界的只有一根细电报线，一下雪就断。一个年轻工程师得自己爬上山崖，一把一把地修那根绳。网络通了以后，家家都能挑不同的频道，孩子们整晚都待在家里。我奶奶说她还是喜欢那台旧收音机，可她还是陪着我们看。那根电缆比任何一条路带来的改变都大。她床边至今还放着她那台小收音机。"},"cap":{"tw":[["capital",0],["control",2],["blue",6],["crown",0],["lie",2],["lift",6],["jacket",0]],"en":"He wore a blue cap and a heavy jacket, and would lift it only to greet the women at the gate. The whole capital could be seen from the roof of the little plant where he worked, and he liked to stand there in the evening. He had no crown and no control over anything beyond his own bench, but he never complained. When he was tired he would lie down on the old bench for ten minutes. People said he had a quiet pride, and that no one could take the cap from him. He kept that cap for thirty years.","zh":"他戴一顶蓝帽子，穿一件厚夹克，只有跟大门口的女人们打招呼时才把帽子抬一抬。从他干活的那座小厂屋顶上能望见整座都城，晚上他喜欢站在那儿。他没有什么王冠，除了自己那张工作台，也管不了别的什么，可他从不抱怨。累了，他就在旧长凳上躺十分钟。人们说他有种安静的骄傲，谁也拿不走他那顶帽子。那顶帽子他戴了三十年。"},"captain":{"tw":[["master",0],["head",2],["knight",6],["skipper",0],["officer",2],["squad",6],["chieftain",0]],"en":"The old skipper had been master of the boat for thirty years, and everyone still called him captain. He was no knight and wore no crown, but he knew every rock in the bay by heart. As head of a small squad of six, he had once been an officer in the port guard before he took to the water. When the weather turned, he would stand at the front and give his orders slowly. The men said a good chieftain was one who ate last and slept least. He never raised his voice, and the crew never doubted him. That was the whole of his command.","zh":"那位老船长当了这条船三十年的主人，大家还是叫他船长。他算不上什么骑士，也没有王冠，可海湾里每一块礁石他都记在心里。他领着一个六人的小班，在改行下水之前，曾在港口警卫队当过军官。天气一变，他就站到船头，慢慢地发号施令。船员们说，真正的好头领是最后一个吃饭、睡得最少的人。他从不抬高嗓门，船员也从没怀疑过他。他的威信就全在这儿了。"},"challenge":{"tw":[["dispute",0],["call",3],["demand",2],["win",6],["question",3],["invite",2],["tough",6]],"en":"The teacher put a tough question on the board and invited anyone to try it. It was a real challenge, and at first nobody moved. Then a boy who rarely spoke raised his hand and called out an answer. A short dispute followed, because another student would demand to know how he had got there. The teacher said the argument itself was the point, and that a good learner should always question a result, even a winning one. In the end the whole class had to work it out together, and the boy was the first to say he had been wrong.","zh":"老师在黑板上写下一道难题，请谁愿意就来试试。这是个真正的挑战，一开始没人动。后来一个很少开口的男孩举手，喊出了一个答案。接着是一小段争辩，因为另一个学生非要问他是怎么想出来的。老师说，这场争论本身就是重点，一个好学生就该对结果存疑，哪怕是赢了的结果。最后全班一起把它解了出来，而第一个说自己错了的，正是那个男孩。"},"chest":{"tw":[["breast",0],["bosom",3],["box",2],["forehead",6],["dresser",0],["coffer",3],["neck",6]],"en":"In the attic stood an old wooden chest that had come down through the family. My grandmother kept her letters in a smaller box on the dresser, and her rings in a little coffer inside the chest. She held one letter close to her breast whenever she read it, and then put her hand to her forehead as if remembering. When she was tired she would press the chest to her neck and close her eyes. She said an old bosom holds more than money ever could. Nobody in the family would ever sell it. It still smells faintly of the cedar it was made from.","zh":"阁楼上放着一只祖上传下来的旧木箱。奶奶把她的信收在梳妆台上一只小盒子里，把戒指放在箱子里面一个小匣中。她读信时总把信贴在胸前，然后把手按上额头，仿佛在回忆什么。累了，她就把箱子贴在脖子上，闭上眼睛。她说，一个老胸怀里装的东西，比钱多得多。家里谁也不会把它卖掉。它至今还隐隐带着做箱子那棵雪松的味道。"},"chop":{"tw":[["hack",0],["axe",3],["cut",2],["onion",6],["mince",3],["move",2],["grate",6]],"en":"My father would chop the onion on the old board while the soup was on the stove. He kept a heavy axe on the wall, though it never left the shed, and a small knife to cut the meat. He liked to mince a few things fine and then grate the cheese over the top. I would stand at the door and watch his hands move, quick and sure. When he had hacked a big piece into smaller ones, he would smile and say the trick was patience. He never let anyone else near the board. That board had fed us for thirty years.","zh":"汤在灶上煮着的时候，我爸会在旧案板上切洋葱。墙上挂着一把沉斧头，虽然它从不出棚子，另有一把小刀切肉。他喜欢把几样东西剁得细细的，再往上面擦些奶酪。我会站在门口看他的手动来动去，又快又稳。他把一大块砍成小块以后，会笑着说诀窍就是耐心。他从不让人靠近那块案板。那块板养活了我们三十年。"},"coach":{"tw":[["train",0],["car",2],["team",6],["carriage",2],["drive",2],["quarterback",6],["tutor",0]],"en":"My sister was asked to coach the school team in the spring, though she had never been a quarterback herself. She would drive to the field in an old car and stay until dark. Each week she would train the younger players and act as a tutor to the ones who were struggling. In the corner of the yard stood an old carriage that her grandfather had once used on the farm. She liked to say the word she answered to had two meanings, and that she was happy with both. Her whole team improved in a single season. The carriage stayed right where it was.","zh":"那年春天，我姐被请去当校队的教练，尽管她自己从没打过四分卫。她会开着一辆旧车去场地，一直待到天黑。每周她都带那些年纪小的队员训练，还充当几个跟不上的人的辅导老师。院角停着一辆旧马车，是她爷爷当年在农场上用过的。她总爱说，她答应的那个称呼有两个意思，两个她都乐意。她那支队伍一个赛季就整个变了样。那辆马车就停在原处没动。"},"cop":{"tw":[["arrest",0],["steal",2],["kid",6],["hook",0],["seize",2],["guy",6],["nail",0]],"en":"On our street there was one cop who knew every family by name. He would seize a bike that someone had tried to steal and hook it back on its rack before the owner even noticed. A little kid once came to him crying, and he knelt down to hear the whole story. He said the hardest part of the job was not the arrest, but telling a mother that her son had been picked up. He never had to use his hands, only his voice. People said he could nail a lie with one slow question. The guy retired last year, and the street feels quieter.","zh":"我们这条街上有个警察，家家户户的名字他都叫得出来。他会追回一辆有人想偷的自行车，在车主发现之前就把它挂回车架。有个小孩哭着跑来找他，他就蹲下来把整件事听完。他说这活儿最难的地方不是抓人，而是去告诉一位母亲，她儿子被带走了。他从来不用动手，只用声音。人们说，他一个问题慢慢地问出来，就能把谎话钉死。那个家伙去年退了休，街上安静了许多。"},"crawl":{"tw":[["creep",0],["feel",2],["wander",6],["cringe",0],["swim",2],["window",6],["fawn",0]],"en":"The baby had just learned to crawl, and she would creep across the floor after the cat. She liked to pull herself up to the window and look out. Sometimes she would wander into the kitchen and we would have to bring her back. I would feel a little cringe each time she reached the sharp corner, but she never cried. In summer we took her to the pool and let her swim with a ring round her waist. A young fawn had once come to the garden fence, and she watched it without a sound. Those were the fastest months of my life.","zh":"孩子刚学会爬，会跟着猫在地板上爬来爬去。她喜欢撑着爬到窗边往外看。有时她会一路爬到厨房，我们得把她抱回来。每次她够到那个尖角，我心里就一紧，可她却从不哭。夏天我们带她去泳池，让她套着救生圈游泳。有一次一只小鹿来到花园的篱笆边，她一声不响地看着它。那是我一生里过得最快的几个月。"},"creep":{"tw":[["crawl",0],["travel",2],["lurch",6],["mouse",0],["walk",2],["drift",6],["sneak",0]],"en":"A mouse would creep out from behind the box as soon as the house grew quiet. It would crawl along the wall, then lurch forward and drift across the open floor before it vanished again. Once it tried to sneak up to the table, but the chair moved and it froze. He would walk to the kitchen slowly, without a light, and wait. He said fear can travel faster than any small animal. In the morning we found a trail of crumbs leading to a gap under the door. Nobody wanted to kill it. It had been living under that box longer than we had lived there.","zh":"屋子一静下来，那只老鼠就从箱子后面悄悄爬出来。它沿着墙根爬，往前一窜，再慢慢滑过空地板，然后又不见了。有一次它想偷偷爬到桌边，可椅子一动，它就僵住了。我爸会不开灯慢慢走到厨房，在那儿等。他说恐惧传得比任何小动物都快。早上我们发现一道面包屑的痕迹，一直通到门下的缝里。谁也不想弄死它。它在那只箱子底下住的时间，比我们住在这儿还长。"},"curl":{"tw":[["lock",0],["play",2],["slink",6],["roll",0],["wind",2],["poke",6],["wave",0]],"en":"My cousin had a thick curl of hair that would never stay in place, and she would wind it round one finger while she talked. Her little dog liked to roll on the carpet and play with a ball of wool. When the wind came through the open window, a wave of it went across the room and lifted the papers. The dog would slink under the table and poke its nose out only when it was sure. She kept a small lock of hair from her grandmother, tied with a thread. She said some things you keep, and some you let go.","zh":"我表妹有一绺怎么也不服帖的厚卷发，说话时她会把它绕在手指上。她那只小狗爱在地毯上打滚，玩一团毛线。风从敞着的窗户吹进来时，屋里像过了一道浪，把纸都掀了起来。狗会溜到桌子底下，等确定没事了才把鼻子探出来。她把奶奶的一小缕头发收在盒子里，用一根线扎着。她说，有些东西要留着，有些要放开。"}}});
